.Ss "1.1 Description"
IMP is a simple higher-level language intended primarily for system
programming [Irons 1970], [Bilofsky 1972]. It provides language facilities
roughly at the level of FORTRAN II and leaves the programmer the flexibility
of machine language programming: the address of any variable can be
taken, memory can be addressed directly, and a word can be treated as
bytes and bit fields.
IMP72 is the version of IMP defined by the compiler of Walt Bilofsky
for the PDP-10.
This implementation translates the same language to native code on 64-bit
Unix systems, and provides the following facilities:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
Extensibility.
The user may specify extensions to the syntax and semantics of the language
in forms ranging from simple
.Dq macros
to productions which generate calls to compiler code-generating routines.
More efficient object code may easily be specified for special cases.
.It 2.
Floating point capabilities.
A
.Ic REAL
data type and floating point arithmetic are provided; a
.Ic REAL
is an IEEE 754 double.
.It 3.
Byte and bit field manipulation.
.It 4.
No reserved words in the syntax.
.It 5.
Syntactic error correction and admissibility of ambiguous syntax.
.It 6.
Native code.
The compiler writes LLVM IR, makes an object file for the host or for
another system, and links it with the library of the runtime.
.It 7.
Calls to
.Li C
functions, and routines that
.Li C
can call, through the
.Li C
calling convention (Section 2.4).
.El
.Ss "1.2 Implementation"
The compiler is a program in IMP72 and compiles itself.
Almost none of the language is built into it: a small kernel (the lexer,
the parser, the syntax graph, the code tree matcher and the semantic
stack, Section 4) starts empty, and the file of syntax statements
.Ic SYNTAX.IMC
defines the language, each statement carrying the semantic routines
that generate its code (Section 2.3).
The code is generated for an abstract machine of 64-bit words; the compiler
translates it to LLVM IR and makes the object file with the LLVM libraries
and links it with lld, all in its own process.
The compiler never runs a
.Ar C
compiler or an assembler.
.Pp
The compiler and the runtime library run on Linux, macOS, FreeBSD, NetBSD
and OpenBSD, on the processors that LLVM supports there (x86-64, i386,
aarch64, armv7, powerpc64le, riscv64, s390x, loongarch64).
Programs may be compiled for another system than the one the compiler
runs on (Section 3.1.3).
.Pp
The compiler is provided under the licence of Section 5.
The compiler is called with the command imp, described in Section 3.
.Ss "1.3 How to Use IMP in One Easy Lesson"
This section is intended to give the experienced programmer the flavor
of IMP so that he may write simple programs without having to read the
entire manual.
.Pp
IMP is similar to FORTRAN II in power, and a bit like ALGOL in flavor.
It has no block structure or reserved words, and all variables are global
to all programs in a compilation.
Statements may be grouped, using parentheses.
Variables need not be declared unless they are arrays.
Arrays are declared by, e.g.,
.Li "FOO IS 50 LONG"
and subscripts written, e.g.,
.Li FOO[I+3\&] .
The assignment operator is
.Li :=
(one token) and the statement separator is
.Dq Li \&; .
The words of the language are written in capital letters; names are
case sensitive, so that
.Ic X
and
.Li x
are two variables.
All binary arithmetic operators are of equal precedence, and operations
are performed from right to left (as in APL).
.Li :=
is of the same precedence as the arithmetic operators and also is performed
from right to left.
Relational operators are evaluated after arithmetic operators.
.Pp
Statements may be labeled by prefixing them with
.Li LABEL\&: .
The transfer of control is
.Li "GO TO LABEL"
and the conditional is
.Li A=>B
or
.Li "A=>B ELSE C" ,
where
.Ar B
and
.Ar C
are statements or groups of statements (in parentheses), and
.Ar A
is an expression (0 is false, non-zero is true), perhaps containing
a relational operator
.Li "(EQ, NE, GE, GT, LE, LT\&)" .
The basic iteration construct is
.Li "A FOR B IN C,D,E" ,
where
.Ar A
is a group of statements (in parentheses),
.Ar B
is a variable, and
.Ar C ,
.Ar D
and
.Ar E
are expressions.
.Ar A
is executed for values of
.Ar B
from
.Ar C ,
incremented by
.Ar D ,
to
.Ar E .
.Pp
An IMP source file consists of a number of statements, in free format.
The file is terminated by
.Dq Li %% .
The last statement executed should be
.Li FINI(0\&) ,
which closes the files and exits from the program.
A program that prints the sum of the integers from 1 to 10 is
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SUM:=0;
(SUM:=SUM+I) FOR I IN 1,1,10;
PRINT STG 0,'The sum is ',IGR 0,SUM,/;
FINI(0)
%%
.Ed
.Pp
To compile the IMP program on file
.Li sum.imp ,
call the compiler by the command
.Li "imp sum.imp" ,
which makes the executable program
.Li "a.out; imp \-o sum sum.imp"
names it
.Li sum .
Running it prints
.Bd -literal -offset indent
The sum is 55
.Ed
.Ss "1.4 Differences between IMP72 on Unix and the Original IMP72"
The language is the language of the original compiler for the PDP-10
under TOPS-10.
What differs comes from the machine and from the runtime library.
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
The word is 64 bits.
Integers are 64-bit two’s complement numbers, addresses are byte addresses
and
.Li A[I\&]
scales the subscript by 8.
The original’s word was 36 bits.
.It 2.
Assignment is the token
.Li := ,
where the original wrote
.Li _ .
The character
.Li _
is a letter of names.
Names are case sensitive, as the original’s are, and the words of the
language are the capital-letter forms.
.It 3.
A
.Ic REAL
is an IEEE 754 double, not the PDP-10’s 36-bit floating point number.
.It 4.
A character is a byte and text is UTF-8.
A string constant is a sequence of bytes ended by a zero byte, not five
7-bit characters to a word.
.It 5.
Byte pointers to characters are byte addresses; the halves of a word
are 32 bits.
.It 6.
Files are Unix files, and a file name is at most seven characters, with
an extension of at most seven.
Section 2.2.7.
.It 7.
The program is compiled to native code and linked to an executable by
the compiler.
The compiler is called imp and takes options like a
.Ar C
compiler.
Section 3.
.It 8.
The registers
.Li 0R
to
.Li 15R
are 64-bit integer variables of each routine,
.Ic EXECUTE
runs an instruction of the abstract machine, and
.Ic CALLI
has the few calls of Section 2.2.9.
.It 9.
.Ic CCALL ,
.Ic EXTERNAL
and the rules for
.Ic COMMON
let a program call
.Li C
and be called from
.Li C .
Section 2.4.
.It 10.
The syntax file defines the same language as the original’s, with the
operation codes of the abstract machine in the semantic routine calls
instead of the PDP-10’s.
Section 2.3.
.El
.Sh "2 PROGRAMMER’S GUIDE TO IMP72"
.Ss "2.1 Conventions"
.Ss "2.1.1 Lexical"
The language is basically free-form, subject to the following constraints.
Words representing variable names or special words of the language are
delimited by characters that are not letters, digits or the underscore,
including space.
Therefore, spaces may not appear inside words or numbers, and at least
one delimiter must appear between two such words.
All characters having an ASCII character code of
.Li 40B
or less are interpreted as blanks, except when enclosed in !,
.Li #
or
.Li \(aq
signs.
Thus, string constants may contain any character, including tabs and
returns, and returns and line feeds outside !,
.Li #
and
.Li \(aq
signs serve as delimiters.
As a general rule, make the text readable and you will find the words
properly distinguished.
.Pp
Identifiers consist of letters, digits and the underscore, and are case
sensitive:
.Ic COUNT ,
.Li Count
and
.Li count
are three names.
The words of the language
.Li "(PRINT, SUBR, IS, ...\&)"
are written in capital letters, so that
.Li print
is a name.
A byte above 127 is a letter, so that the UTF-8 text of a name is part
of it.
An identifier containing any special character (except !) may be represented
by enclosing the entire name in ! signs; e.g.,
.Li !Funny*id.\&!
(see Section 2.2.1).
.Pp
All characters appearing between
.Li #
signs are treated as comments and ignored by the compiler (except for
.Li #
signs in string constants and between ! signs).
A comment that is not closed by a second
.Li #
extends to the end of the file.
.Ss "2.1.2 Expressions and Statements"
In IMP, no distinction is made between expressions and statements.
Almost everything is an expression, and has a value, although a few
expressions like
.Ic GO TO
.Ar X
do not have a very useful value.
Two consequences of this philosophy are that parentheses may be used
for grouping what are usually called statements in other languages,
and that the statement separator
.Ql \&;
becomes a binary operator (whose value is the second operand).
.Pp
The terms
.Dq statement
and
.Dq expression
will be used almost interchangeably herein.
When the term
.Dq expression
is used, it should be understood that we may be concerned with the value
of the expression, whereas when the term
.Dq statement
appears, we are interested primarily in the effect of executing it,
and only secondarily, if at all, in its value.
.Ss "2.1.3 Machine Language Level Programming"
The identifiers
.Li 0R,1R,...,15R
name sixteen registers.
A register is a 64-bit integer variable.
The main program and each routine have a set of sixteen of their own,
and a register is 0 when the routine is entered.
A call does not change the registers of the caller.
The compiler’s own temporaries are not these registers: the machine
registers are assigned by the LLVM code generator.
For example, the statements
.Li "1R:=5; 2R:=1R+3; PRINT IGR 0,2R,/"
print 8.
.Pp
The statement
.Ic EXECUTE
(Section 2.2.9) executes an instruction word of the abstract machine
for which the compiler generates code, and syntax statements (Section
2.3) can use every operation of that machine.
.Ss "2.1.4 Program Structure and Scope of Variables"
Program structure is more or less like that of the FORTRAN system.
Thus a main program, if any, comes first, then subroutines, if any,
follow.
The main program is the statements before the first
.Ic SUBR ;
it is compiled to the function
.Li impmain
that the runtime library’s
.Li main
calls (Section 2.4.4).
.Pp
In contrast to the FORTRAN convention, all variables in IMP are global
to the entire set of subroutines compiled at the same time, except that
formal arguments of a subroutine are local within that subroutine.
A set of subroutines compiled at the same time is one source file.
That is, any two references to the same variable name within a single
IMP source file will refer to the same memory location, even if the
references are within different subroutines.
A variable that is not declared
.Ic COMMON
belongs to its source file: another file has a variable of its own with
the same name, and
.Li C
cannot name it.
The
.Ic COMMON
variables are the ones shared between files and with
.Li C
(Section 2.4.3).
However, references to formal arguments of subroutines will have the
effect of references to the memory location containing the actual argument
in the call of the subroutine.
Thus references to formal subroutine arguments will not affect variables
of the same name in other subprograms in the same compilation.
.Pp
The declaration
.Ic LOCAL
is provided to defeat the fact that variables are normally global to
the entire file.
An identifier which is declared to be
.Ic LOCAL
will be replaced throughout the subroutine in which it is so declared
by a unique name, which will not be used outside the subroutine.
The identifier will therefore appear local to the subroutine.
A
.Ic LOCAL
variable is static: it keeps its value from one call to the next.
.Pp
Because every variable is static, and the arguments of a call are evaluated
into temporaries that are also static, a subroutine cannot usefully
call itself.
Recursion is programmed with a stack of the program’s own.
.Pp
IMP uses the calling convention of FORTRAN, in which the arguments are
addresses (Section 2.2.5), and the
.Li C
calling convention for the names it exports (Section 2.4).
IMP and
.Li C
programs may call each other.
.Ss "2.1.5 Compiler Version"
The version of the compiler is printed by the command
.Li "imp \-\-version"
(the text
.Li "imp 0.10.0" )
and appears, with the date, in the banner of the interactive mode (Section
3.1.2).
An extended compiler (Section 3.4) has the version number of the compiler
it was made from.
.Ss "2.2 The Expressions"
The expressions of the language are listed here.
Expressions may be grouped explicitly by parentheses.
Where parentheses are omitted, the following is the order in which subexpressions
are evaluated:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
Unary operators and functions.
.It 2.
Binary operators except
.Dq Li \&;
(but including
.Dq Li := ) .
.It 3.
Relational operators.
.It 4.
Conditionals.
.It 5.
.Dq Li \&; .
.El
.Pp
When two operators of equal precedence appear consecutively in an expression,
the rightmost is performed first (as in APL).
E.g.,
.Li A*B+C
is evaluated as
.Li A*(B+C\&) .
.Sy EXCEPTION :
the order of evaluation of relational operators on the same level -
e.g.,
.Li A<B
.Ic GE
.Ar C
- is not defined.
.Pp
In
.Dq Li "A; B" ,
.Ar A
is evaluated before
.Ar B .
In a conditional, the implicand will be evaluated after the condition
is evaluated, if at all.
Other than the restrictions listed above, IMP places no restrictions
on the order of evaluation of arguments of operators.
.Pp
In the following table, if no value is specified for an expression,
then the value of that expression is not defined and/or not useful.
.Ss "2.2.1 Variables and Constants"
.Bd -literal -offset indent
