.It Li \(aqABCdef\(aq
String constants: a sequence of bytes (the text is UTF-8) followed by
a zero byte, stored in successive words.
The value of a string of eight bytes or fewer is the word that holds
it, with the first byte in the least significant byte, which is the
order of the bytes in memory.
Thus after
.Li S:=\(aqABC\(aq
the variable
.Ar S
holds the string
.Ic ABC ,
and
.Li "PRINT STG 0,S"
prints it.
The value of a longer string is its first eight bytes; the address of
the string is
.Li LOC(\(aqtext\(aq\&) ,
which is what a
.Ar C
function expects (Section 2.4).
The character
.Li \(aq
is represented within a string by two successive quotes:
.Li \(aq\(aq .
Thus, the string consisting of one single quote would be written
.Li \(aq\(aq\(aq\(aq
(but would print as
.Li \(aq ) .
.It Li "!Any thing.\&!"
Special identifier: any string of characters not containing a
.Ql \&! ,
bracketed by \&'!'s.
The string of characters is considered to be a single identifier or
symbol.
In particular, the following interpretations are made:
.Bl -bullet -compact -offset indent
.It
One special character is recognized as itself.
E.g.,
.Li !+\&!
is the same as
.Li + .
.It
Any string starting with a single quote is recognized as the string
constant containing the remainder of the string.
E.g.,
.Li !\(aqDon\(aqt\&!
is the string constant
.Dq Don’t .
.It
Any other string is interpreted as an identifier whose name is exactly
the string appearing between the ! signs.
This allows variable names containing special characters.
In the object file the name is a symbol in which each byte that is not
a letter, a digit or an underscore is written as an underscore and its
two hexadecimal digits:
.Li !Funny*id.\&!
is the symbol
.Li Funny_2aid_2e .
