.Ss "2.2.4 Control Expressions"
.Bl -tag -width 15n -compact
.It Li A=>B
If
.Ar A
is not 0, evaluate
.Ar B
(Read as
.Dq Li "A implies B" ) .
Thus,
.Li K=>X:=1
does nothing if
.Ar K
is zero, or sets
.Ar X
to 1 if
.Ar K
is non-zero.
.Li X<Y=>(P:=1;Q:=2\&)
changes
.Ar P
and
.Ar Q
if
.Ar X
is less than
.Ar Y ,
otherwise does nothing.
.Sy "NOTA BENE" :
If
.Ar A
is a relational expression, it need not, and for best object code should
not, be enclosed in parentheses.
If
.Ar A
is a constant, or an expression involving only constants, then the code
generated by the compiler for the expression
.Li A=>B
will consist either of
.Ar B ,
if
.Ar A
is non-zero, or nothing at all, if
.Ar A
is zero.
This allows code to be compiled conditionally if, for example,
.Ar A
is an identifier defined by syntax to be a constant.
In the case that
.Ar B
is not compiled due to
.Ar A
being a constant 0, any tags contained within
.Ar B
will not be defined, but declarations contained in
.Ar B
will take effect.
.It Li A=>B Ic ELSE Ar C
