.Ar B
equal to
.Ar C ,
.Li "C+D, C+2*D" , . . . ,
.Ar E ;
the
.Ic TO
form for
.Ar B
equal to
.Li 0,1,...,E ,
and the
.Ic FROM
form for
.Ar B
equal to
.Li E,E\-1,...,0 .
In every case the expression
.Ar A
is evaluated at least once regardless of the values of
.Ar C ,
.Ar D
and
.Ar E .
In the
.Ic IN
case when
.Ar D
is not a constant,
.Ar B
must exactly reach the value of
.Ar E .
E.g., in
.Bd -literal -offset indent
I:=2;
(A[J]:=0) FOR J IN 0,I,9
.Ed
.Pp
the loop will never terminate.
In all other cases, the loop terminates when
.Ar B
passes the terminal value.
.Ar C ,
.Ar D
and
.Ar E
may be any expressions, but in the
.Ic IN
and
.Ic TO
cases
.Ar D
and
.Ar E
will be evaluated once per iteration of the loop.
The
.Ic FROM
case generates the most efficient code and is therefore to be preferred.
If control is transferred from within the loop, the value of the index
variable
.Ar B
is preserved.
The value of
.Ar B
following normal termination of the loop is undefined.
.Bl -bullet -compact -offset indent
.It
The special cases
.Li "(A[I]:=B[I]) FOR I TO/FROM V" ,
where
.Ar A
and
.Ar B
are integer arrays and
.Ar V
is a single variable or constant, are compiled as a block transfer instruction.
The user is cautioned that in the
.Ic FROM
case, if
.Ar A
and
.Ar B
overlap, the code produced will be incorrect.
In more general cases, block transfers may be specified by the
.Ic MOVE
construct (Section 2.2.9).
.El
.It Ic WHILE Ar A Ic DO Ar B
Repeatedly evaluates the expression
.Ar A ,
and, if it is nonzero (true), evaluates the expression
.Ar B .
If
.Ar A
is zero (false), control passes to the next statement.
If
.Ar A
is initially zero,
.Ar B
is never executed.
.It Li "A UNTIL B"
This expression evaluates the expression
.Ar A ,
then evaluates
.Ar B
and if
.Ar B
is false (zero) it repeats.
Even if
.Ar B
is initially true,
.Ar A
is always executed at least once.
.It Li ((A=B\&) Ic AND Pf \&( Li C=D ) ) Li => Ar E
.It Li ((A=B\&) Ic OR Pf \&( Li C=D ) ) Li => Ar E Ic ELSE Ar F
A condition may be several relations joined by
.Ic AND
or
.Ic OR ,
each relation in parentheses, in a conditional, after
.Ic WHILE
or after
.Ic UNTIL .
The relations are evaluated from left to right and no more of them than
necessary are evaluated.
A condition of one relation in parentheses alone, as in
.Li "(A=1) => S" ,
is a syntactic ambiguity: write
.Li "A=1 => S" .
.It Ic GO TO Ar E
Transfer control to the tag
.Ar E .
A
.Ic GO TO
in a subroutine may name a tag of the main program: control passes there,
and the subroutines called on the way are left.
.It Ic GO TO Pf \&( Li L0,L1,...,LN ) Ar E
This expression transfers control to the label Li, where i is the value
of expression
.Ar E ,
and L0,...,LN are identifiers.
If
.Li E<0
or
.Li E>N ,
control continues with the statement after the
.Ic GO TO .
.It Li T:A
The expression
.Ar A
is tagged with the label
.Ar T .
Two uses of this are:
.Ic GO TO
.Ar T
transfers control to this point in the program, and, when
.Ar A
is a
.Ic DATA
statement, the variable
.Ar T
is the first word of the data (Section 2.2.8).
To insert a tag where an expression does not immediately follow, such
as before a
.Ql \&) ,
write
.Li TAG:0 .
.El
.Ss "2.2.5 Programs, Subprograms and Subprogram Calls"
.Bl -tag -width 15n -compact
.It Li A%%
An IMP program consists of an IMP expression followed by two
.Li %
signs.
The
.Li %
signs terminate the input file as far as the compiler is concerned.
The file
.Li sum.imp
of Section 1.3 is such a program.
.It Ic SUBR Li A(B,C\&) Ic IS Ar D
Define a subroutine (or function) named
.Ar A ,
with formal arguments
.Ar B
and
.Ar C .
The value of the subroutine is
.Ar D ,
(unless the subroutine is exited via a
.Ic RETURN
statement, q.v.).
The normal exit from the subroutine is by
.Dq running off the end
of the expression
.Ar D .
Example:
.Pp
.Dl "SUBR ABS(A) IS (A<0=>\-A ELSE A)\&;"
.Pp
is a subroutine whose value is the absolute value of its argument.
With this definition
.Li P:=ABS(Q\&)
calls the subroutine
.Ic ABS
and sets
.Ar P
to the value of
.Ar Q .
The arguments of a subroutine
.Dq share
the memory location of the actual arguments with which the subroutine
is called; that is, changing the value of one of the parameters in the
subroutine will change the value of the variable in the calling program
which corresponds to that argument.
A subroutine is a
.Li C
function that takes pointers: the call
.Li F(A1,A2,...,AN\&)
passes the address of each argument, and an argument that is not a variable
(a constant or an expression) is stored in a temporary whose address
is passed.
The subroutine refers to its i-th argument through the i-th pointer
and returns a 64-bit integer, so that the
.Li C
declaration of
.Li "SUBR ADD2(A,B) IS A+B"
is
.Li "int64_t ADD2(int64_t *a, int64_t *b\&)" .
A subprogram called with more arguments than it expects is not harmed.
If it is called with fewer, the parameters that were not passed are
undefined.
Functions return their value as the 64-bit result; the word of a
.Ic REAL
is the bits of the double.
The name of a subroutine is the symbol of the function in the object
file, as written (names are case sensitive).
The routines are not reentrant (Section 2.1.4).
.It Ic SUBR No A(\&) Ic IS Ar D
Define a subroutine (or function) named A with no formal arguments,
and with value
.Ar D .
.It Ic RETURN Ar E
Return from the subroutine in which this statement appears.
The value returned by the subroutine is
.Ar E .
.It Li A(B,C,D\&)
Execute the function A with parameters
.Li B,C,D :
The value of this expression is the value of the function.
(Since there is no distinction in IMP between functions and subroutines,
this expression is also used to call A as a subroutine with the indicated
parameters.)
.It Li A(\&)
Execute the function A with no parameters.
.El
.Ss "2.2.6 Byte Access"
.Bl -tag -width 15n -compact
.It Li A<B,C>
This object is called a
.Dq byte .
.Ar A
is a variable, either simple or subscripted;
.Ar B
and
.Ar C
are any expressions.
.Li A<B,C>
designates the portion of the word A consisting of
.Ar B
bits, located
.Ar C
bits from the right hand side of the word.
Thus, e.g.,
.Li A<12,12>
designates the 12 bits whose lowest bit is bit 12 of
.Ar A ,
and
.Li A<1,63>
is the sign bit.
A byte may be used as an operand, or have a value stored in it.
Example: the program fragment
.Bd -literal -offset indent
A:=3;
(A<9,I+9>:=3+A<9,I>) FOR I IN 0,9,18;
.Ed
has the effect of setting A to
.Li 14011006003B
(octal).
.Pp
The constructs
.Li A<R>
and
.Li A<L>
designate the right and left half words of
.Ar A .
A half word is 32 bits.
They are equivalent to
.Li A<32,0>
and
.Li A<32,32>
respectively.
.It Ic BYTEP Li A<I,J>
This expression has the value of a byte pointer to the designated byte.
Its usefulness lies in the ability to write
.Li "X:=BYTEP A<B,C>"
and do byte access by referencing
.Ar X ,
as explained in the next paragraph.
If
.Ar I
and
.Ar J
are not constants, the byte pointer will be computed with their values
at the time the statement is executed and will not be reevaluated if
.Ar I
and
.Ar J
change value later.
There are two kinds of pointer.
.Ic BYTEP
.Li A<7,36>
and
.Ic BYTEP
.Li A<8,64> ,
with constants for the size and position, make a pointer to characters:
the byte address of the byte before the first byte of
.Ar A ,
a byte being a character.
Any other
.Ic BYTEP
makes the pointer to a bit field: a word with the size of the field
in bits 58 to 63, the position in bits 52 to 57, and in the remaining
bits the byte address of the word that holds the field.
.It Li <X>
.Ar X
must contain a byte pointer.
(This may be accomplished by
.Li "X:=BYTEP A<I,J>" . )
Then
.Li <X>
refers to the byte designated by the byte pointer.
.It Li <+X>
.Ar X
must contain a byte pointer.
When
.Li <+X>
is evaluated, it increments the byte pointer, and its value is the byte
then pointed to.
Incrementing a byte pointer to characters means that it points to the
next byte of memory.
Incrementing a pointer to
.Li A<I,J>
means that the pointer will now point to
.Li A<I,J\-I>
if
.Ar J
.Ic GE
.Ar I ,
or to
.Li A[1]<I,64\-I>
if
.Li J<I .
Example: Suppose the memory locations starting at
.Ic STG
contain a string (a character to a byte, a zero byte at the end).
Then the following program fragment unpacks
.Ic STG
into the locations starting at
.Ic UNP ,
one character per word (remember, the string terminates with a 0 character):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
STG IS 2 LONG; UNP IS 16 LONG;
STG[0]:='HELLO';
X:=BYTEP STG<7,36>; I:=-1;
LOOP: (UNP[I:=I+1]:=<+X>)=>GO TO LOOP;
.Ed
After it
.Li UNP[0\&]
to
.Li UNP[5\&]
hold 72, 69, 76, 76, 79 and 0, the codes of the characters.
.Pp
Byte pointers may also be stored into, with or without first being incremented:
.Li <+X>:=I .
Only the low 8 bits of a character are stored.
The characters of a UTF-8 text are its bytes.
.It Li IDPB(A,B\&)
.It Li ILDB(A\&)
.Li IDPB(A,B\&)
is the same as
.Li <+B>:=A ,
and
.Li ILDB(A\&)
is the same as
.Li <+A> :
they step the byte pointer and store A in the byte, or have the value
of the byte.
.El
.Ss "2.2.7 Input/Output"
.Bl -tag -width 15n -compact
.It Ic PRINT Li A,B,C
.It Ic READ Li A,B,C
.Ar A ,
.Ar B ,
.Ar C ,
etc., are a list of expressions to be transferred from/to input/output
files, with format specifiers mixed in.
Any expression may appear in a
.Ic PRINT
list; if an expression other than a simple or subscripted variable appears
in a
.Ic READ
list the effect will be to read into a temporary location.
The format specifiers are listed below.
The order in which the items of a list are evaluated is not specified;
write
.Li "PRINT <+P>,<+P>"
as two statements.
.Pp
The value of a
.Ic PRINT
statement is the number of the last column on a line that was printed
into.
In particular, the value of
.Ic PRINT
.Li /
is 0.
The value of a
.Ic READ
statement is -1 if an attempt has been made to read past the end of
the file, 1 if an attempt has been made to read past the end of a line,
and 0 otherwise.
.Pp
If no file is explicitly specified, output is to file
.Ic PO ,
the standard output, and input is from file
.Ic PI ,
the standard input.
There is no rule against using many statements to print or read one
line; the only thing that terminates a line is a
.Li / .
A format, once specified, remains in effect until another specification
is encountered, even through several different
.Ic PRINT
or
.Ic READ
statements.
Changing the
.Ic PRINT
format or file does not affect the
.Ic READ
format or file, and vice versa.
A line is held in a buffer of 1023 characters; a longer line is cut.
.Pp
In printing, if a data item is presented that is too large for the specified
field width, a field just large enough to contain it is used.
Consequently, if a field width of 0 is specified, a field just large
enough to contain each data item will be used.
.Pp
In reading, if the field width specification is a single-character string
(e.g.,
.Ql \&, ) ,
the input field will terminate on the first of a) the specified character;
b) the end of the line; c) 128 input characters.
A field width specification of 0 is equivalent to
.Ql \&, .
A field width that is a number from 1 to 31 is a number of columns.
(A number from 32 to 126 is the code of the character that ends the
field, as a one-character string is, so that a field of 32 or more columns
cannot be asked for by its number.) Blanks are ignored on input (except
in
.Ic STG
conversion and when
.Li "\(aq \(aq"
is the field terminator).
Tabs are converted to a single blank on input.
All fields are limited to 128 characters on input, so that a variable
that receives a
.Ic STG
field must be an array of at least 17 words.
.Pp
Field widths in format specifiers are usually constants but may be any
expression.
.Pp
It is necessary to close out your output files explicitly in your program
some time before it terminates.
This is done by the subroutine call
.Li FINI(I\&) ,
for
.Li I=0
or -1.
This closes all your files, and the output and the input return to the
standard output and the standard input.
If
.Li I=0 ,
.Ic FINI
exits from the program; if
.Li I=\-1 ,
.Ic FINI
returns normally.
A program that ends without
.Ic FINI
still has its complete lines written, since the files are closed when
the program exits, but a line that was not ended by
.Li /
is lost.
.Pp
At most four files may be referred to in IMP I/O. If it is desired to
refer to more, one of the previous files must be explicitly closed using
.Ic FINI
(or close all of them with FINI(-1)).
The standard input and the standard output are not counted.
A file that cannot be opened, and a fifth file, stop the program with
a message on the standard error and the exit status 1.
.It Li "Format Specifiers\&:"
.It Ic IGR Ar N
Conversion is in base 10, in a field
.Ar N
positions wide.
On output, leading zeros are suppressed, and a leading minus sign is
printed for negative numbers.
On input, the field is a decimal number with an optional sign.
.It Ic OCT Ar N
Conversion is in base 8, unsigned (i.e., negative numbers print with
leading 7’s: -1 is 1777777777777777777777), in a field
.Ar N
positions wide.
On output, leading zeros are printed.
If
.Ar N
is greater than 12 and the number is less than
.Li 8**12 ,
exactly the leftmost N-12 positions will be blank.
.It Ic STG Ar N
Conversion is in bytes, in a field
.Ar N
wide.
On output, if
.Ar N
is 0 characters are printed until a 0 byte is encountered.
Otherwise, characters are printed until a 0 byte is encountered or until
.Ar N
characters have been printed, and a shorter string is preceded by blanks
to fill the field.
On input, the field is stored as bytes ended by a zero byte.
.It Ic FLT Li M.N
Prints a floating point number (a
.Ic REAL )
in a field
.Ar M
columns wide, with
.Ar N
digits to the right of the decimal point.
If
.Ar N
is 0, no decimal point is printed.
If the number will not fit in the field as specified, scientific notation
is used.
If
.Ar M
is negative, scientific notation is always used in a field -M columns
wide.
(IMP uses
.Li \(dq
to mean
.Sq times 10 to the . )
Exception: if
.Ar M
is 0, a field just wide enough to contain the number is used.
There is no 0 before the decimal point: 0.5 prints as \&.500 for
.Li N=3 .
The digits are those of the IEEE double, correctly rounded.
The value printed is a
.Ic REAL :
an integer given to
.Ic FLT
is read as the bits of a double, so write
.Li FLT(I\&)
for an integer
.Ar I .
On input,
.Ic FLT
.Li M.N
is equivalent to
.Ic IGR
.Ar M .
.It Li /
New line.
On output, causes termination of the current line.
If a line is printed but not terminated with a
.Li / ,
then the line will not print.
Successive /'s produce blank lines.
On input, a
.Li /
causes the remainder of the current line to be skipped over.
.It Ic FILE Ar A
.It Ic FILE Li A.B
Specifies the file for input/output.
.Ar A
and
.Ar B
are file name and extension, and may be either string constants or unsubscripted
variables holding a string.
The file is the Unix file
.Li A.B
of the current directory, or
.Li A
when
.Ar B
is absent or 0;
.Ar A
and
.Ar B
are cut to seven characters each.
A
.Ic PRINT
opens the file for writing, and replaces its contents; a
.Ic READ
opens it for reading.
The file stays the one for the output (or the input) until another
.Ic FILE ,
a
.Ic DEVICE
.Ql TTY\&:
or a
.Ic FINI .
Caution:
.Ic FILE
.Pa FOR03.DAT ,
e.g., refers to variables
.Li FOR03
and
.Ic DAT .
He who wrote this probably meant to write
.Ic FILE
.Ql FOR03\(aq.\(aqDAT .
.Pp
Examples: after
.Li A:=101B ,
the statement
.Ic PRINT STG
.Li "1,A,IGR 4,A,OCT 6,A,A,/"
produces the line
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"A  65000101000101"
.Ed
.Li "(PRINT IGR 3,I)FOR I TO 2;PRINT /"
produces the line
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"  0  1  2"
.Ed
.Ic PRINT STG
0, \&'This is a Page
.Li Heading\(aq,/
produces the line
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"This is a Page Heading".
.Ed
The program
.Bd -literal -offset indent
S IS 8 LONG;
PRINT FILE 'out'.'txt',IGR 4,17,STG 0,' apples',/;
FINI(-1);
READ FILE 'out'.'txt',IGR 4,N,STG 0,S;
PRINT IGR 0,N+1,STG 0,S,/;
FINI(0)
%%
.Ed
writes the file
.Li out.txt
and prints
.Bd -literal -offset indent
18 apples
.Ed
.It Ic DEVICE Ar D
.Ar D
may be either a string constant or a variable containing a string value.
.Ic DEVICE
.Ql TTY\&:
makes the standard output (for
.Ic PRINT )
or the standard input (for
.Ic READ )
the file, with no subsequent file specification.
The next file specification will cause the input or output to revert
to that file.
.It Ic IMAGE MODE
This format specification causes subsequent data transfer to be in unformatted
mode, transferring 8-byte words, in the byte order of the machine, between
the file and the variables in the I/O statement list.
.Ar A
.Li /
in image mode ends nothing.
Mixing
.Ic IMAGE MODE
and other format specifications in reading or writing the same file
may produce anomalous results.
.It Ic TAB Ar N
On
.Ic PRINT
only, causes the next character of output to appear in column
.Ar N
(numbering starting with 1).
If printing has already gone past column
.Ar N ,
no action is taken.
.It Ic FILL No \&'c'
On output only, causes leading positions in all fields to be printed
as the specified character instead of being left blank.
Thus, for example,
.Ic FILL
.Ql 0
causes leading zeros to be printed.
.Ic FILL
.Li "\(aq \(aq"
(or
.Ic FILL
0) restores blanks.
This specification, unlike the others, affects all output files, not
just the current one.
.El
.Ss "2.2.8 Declarations; DATA and REMOTE statements"
.Bl -tag -width 15n -compact
.It Ic LET Li I=2R,A=3,BF=B[1\&]
The
.Ic LET
statement provides a convenient way of declaring synonyms.
It consists of the word
.Ic LET ,
followed by a list of synonym declarations of the form
.Li N=V ,
where
.Ar N
is a name and
.Ar V
is a constant, or a simple or subscripted variable.
The effect is that all subsequent appearances of
.Ar N
in the program will be interpreted as if
.Ar V
had been written instead.
.Ic LET
.Li A=B
is in fact just
.Dq syntactic sugaring
for the syntax statement
.Li "<ATOM> ::= A ::=\(dqB\(dq" .
.It Li A,B Ic ARE No 3 Li LONG,COMMON
.It Li 1R Ic IS REGISTER
.It Li FOO,3R Ic IS RELEASED
Declarations: the general form for a declaration is a list of names,
then a list of characteristics to be associated with these names.
The characteristics available are:
.It Ic REAL
Specifies that the variables are real numbers, and all arithmetic performed
on them is to be floating point.
Type conversion between real and integer is always done implicitly.
A real is an IEEE 754 double, held in the 64-bit word.
.It Ic INTEGER
Specifies that the variables are integers, which they are unless declared
.Ic REAL .
.It Ar N Ic LONG
Where
.Ar N
is a constant or constant expression.
The variables are declared to be arrays, and
.Ar N
words are reserved for each of them.
(The
.Ar N
words may be referred to as
.Ar A
(or
.Li A[0\&] ) ,
.Li A[1\&] ,
\&...
.Li A[N\-1\&] . )
.It Ic COMMON
Variables are made common: each is a global symbol of the object file,
spelled as the name, which the files of a program and
.Li C
share.
Every file that uses a
.Ic COMMON
variable declares it, and the declarations name one variable.
The size of a
.Ic COMMON
array is given by
.Ar N
.Ic LONG
in the declaration.
A variable that is not
.Ic COMMON
belongs to its file (Section 2.1.4).
A
.Ic COMMON
variable that nothing in a file uses is not made by that file.
.Ic COMMON
is how an IMP program and a
.Li C
program share data (Section 2.4.3).
.It Ic EXTERNAL
Variables are defined elsewhere, in the
.Li C
library for example:
.Ar X
.Ic IS EXTERNAL
makes
.Ar X
the variable of that name which the linker finds, and nothing is defined
in the file.
.Li LOC(X\&)
is its address (Section 2.4.3).
.It Ic LOCAL
Makes variables local to the current subroutine from this point to the
end of the subroutine, but does not affect the meaning of the identifier
outside the current subroutine.
A
.Ic LOCAL
variable keeps its value from one call to the next.
.It Ic REGISTER
The declaration
.Ar A
.Ic IS REGISTER
makes A a register: like
.Li 0R
to
.Li 15R
it is a variable local to each routine, which is 0 when the routine
is entered and has no address.
A register cannot be declared
.Ic REAL .
Until the declaration
.Ar A
.Ic IS RELEASED
is encountered, all references to A refer to the register.
.It Ic RELEASED
For identifiers which have been bound to a register, that binding is
terminated, and subsequent references to that identifier will refer
to a memory location (a variable of the file).
.It Ic REMOTE Ar S
.Ar S
is any statement.
Has the effect of causing the code for statement
.Ar S
to be inserted not at the point in the program where the
.Ic REMOTE
statement appears, but at the end of the routine.
Useful in quoted semantics where local variables are required, e.g.,
.Ic LOCAL FOO IN
.Dq Li "\&... REMOTE FOO:0; ..\&." .
.It Ic DATA Pf \&( Ar L )
.Ar L
is a list of constant expressions and string constants.
The
.Ic DATA
statement produces data words at that point in the program containing
the values of the constant expressions.
One word is used for each item in
.Ar L ,
except that strings are stored in as many words as are required.
.Ic DATA
statements may be used to preset variables to a value at compile time,
viz.:
.Ic VAR :
.Ic DATA
(3).
A
.Ic DATA
statement may be executed: it does nothing.
The tag of a
.Ic DATA
statement names its first word, so that after
.Ic TBL :
.Li DATA(10,20,30\&)
the elements are
.Li "TBL[0], TBL[1\&]"
and
.Li TBL[2\&] .
.El
.Ss "2.2.9 Miscellaneous Constructs"
.Bl -tag -width 15n -compact
.It Ic EXECUTE Ar E
Executes the instruction contained in the variable
.Ar E .
The instruction is a word of the abstract machine that the compiler
generates code for: the operation in bits 56 to 63 (the numbers are
listed at the head of the file
.Li syntax.imc :
1
.Ic LOAD ,
6
.Ic ADD ,
7
.Ic SUB ,
\&...), the register in bits 52 to 55, an index register in bits 48
to 51, bit 47 set for an immediate operand and the address or number
in the bits 0 to 46.
The registers are
.Li 0R
to
.Li 15R .
A register that the instruction names must be mentioned elsewhere in
the routine, as
.Li 8R
is in the example, because the compiler keeps the registers a routine
mentions.
The program
.Bd -literal -offset indent
8R:=5;
W:=(1 LS 56) OR (8 LS 52) OR (1 LS 47) OR 42;
EXECUTE W;
PRINT IGR 0,8R,/;
W:=(6 LS 56) OR (8 LS 52) OR (1 LS 47) OR 8;
EXECUTE W;
PRINT IGR 0,8R,/;
FINI(0)
%%
.Ed
.Pp
loads 42 into register 8, adds 8 to it, and prints 42 and 50.
The jumps and the floating point operations cannot be executed this
way.
.It Li CALLI(C,V\&)
Calls the library routine
.Ic IMPCALLI
with the code
.Ar C
and the argument
.Ar V .
The code
.Ar C
is a number or an expression.
The value of the construct is the value of the routine.
The calls are (octal) 12, exit (the output is flushed); 14, the date,
as the number
.Pf \&(( Li year\-1964)*12+month\-1)*31+day\-1 ;
23, the time of day in milliseconds; 27, the run time of the program
in milliseconds; and 30, the job number, which is 1.
.It Ic XWD Li A,B
Useful occasionally in system calls.
Has the value of the low 32 bits of
.Ar B
ORed with the low 32 bits of A shifted left 32 bits.
.It Ic IOWD Li A,B
Has the value
.Ic XWD
.Li \-A,B\-1 .
.It Li FIX(A\&)
Has the value of the expression
.Ar A ,
converted to type integer (by truncation toward zero) if it was not
already of that type.
.It Li FLT(A\&)
Has the value of the expression
.Ar A ,
converted to type real if it was not already of that type.
.It Ic MOVE No A Ic THROUGH Ar N Ic TO Ar B
Copies the
.Li N+1
words that start at A to the
.Li N+1
words that start at
.Ar B ,
upward, one word at a time.
It performs the operation
.Li "(B[I]:=A[I]) FOR I TO N" .
.Ar N
is any positive expression (if real, it is fixed).
.Ar A
and
.Ar B
are any expressions having an address, such as simple or subscripted
variables, or [expression].
.El
.Ss "2.3 Syntactic and Semantic Extension"
This section explains the IMP72 facilities for extending the language.
It is possible to add productions to the syntax for the language, defining
the semantics for the new constructs in several ways.
Semantics may be specified in terms of expressions written in the portion
of the language already defined, as explained in Section 2.3.1.
Alternatively, semantics may be performed by a series of function calls
to semantic subroutines contained within the compiler, as documented
in Section 2.3.3.1.
The user may use the subroutines provided, or, if he requires operations
not available from the current set of semantic subroutines, he may as
a last resort go into the compiler to add new ones.
Different semantics may be specified in special cases, as explained
in Section 2.3.3.2, either to perform different operations upon objects
in different contexts, or to generate better object code.
When the semantics for several productions are very similar, they may
be lumped together under a general case, as noted in Section 2.3.3.3
and 2.3.3.4.
.Ss "2.3.1 The Easy Way — Syntactic “Macros”"
This section is an introduction to syntactic extension in IMP72 for
the casual user, and is intended to provide him with sufficient information
to utilize the basic facility without informing to the point of total
confusion.
Some of these features are in fact less restricted than indicated in
this section.
The full truth emerges in subsequent sections.
.Pp
IMP72 contains a facility for defining syntactic macros, or patterns
which the compiler will recognize in a program and generate specific
code for.
An example of a definition for the absolute value function using a syntactic
macro is
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<EXP> ::= ABS ( <A> ) ::= "A<0=>-A ELSE A"
.Ed
.Pp
This statement, when inserted in a program, will cause the compiler
to recognize the construct
.Ic ABS ,
followed by a
.Dq Li \&( ,
followed by any expression, followed by a
.Dq Li \&) ,
and to substitute for it the code enclosed in
.Li \(dq
signs, inserting the actual expression for each instance of A in the
quoted expression.
Thus, writing
.Bd -literal -offset indent
X:=ABS(R+3)
.Ed
.Pp
later in the program would be the same as writing
.Bd -literal -offset indent
X:=(R+3<0=>-(R+3) ELSE R+3)
.Ed
.Pp
A syntax statement fits the following pattern:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<EXP> ::= syntax part ::= semantic part
.Ed
.Pp
The syntax part is the pattern which the compiler is to recognize.
It consists of the names and special symbols in the pattern, and, for
each expression in the pattern, an identifier in angle brackets: e.g.,
.Li <FOO> .
Single characters in quotes (e.g.,
.Ql # )
are interpreted as that character, without the quotes, and the characters
.Li ": ; \(dq <"
and
.Li %
must be quoted if they appear in the syntax part.
.Pp
The semantic part consists of an IMP expression in double quotes, perhaps
preceded by a list of local variables.
It defines the code IMP is to generate when it recognizes an instance
of the syntax part, with the actual expressions in the instance to be
inserted in the quoted expression in place of the identifiers which
appeared in angle brackets in the syntax part.
.Pp
The quoted expression may be preceded with a list of local variables.
For example, notice that the definition of
.Ic ABS
above computes A twice, which may be inefficient if
.Ar A
is an expression.
A more efficient way of doing it is
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<EXP> ::= ABS ( <A> ) ::= LOCAL R IN "R IS REGISTER;
                    (R:=A)<0=>-R ELSE R"
.Ed
.Pp
This definition computes A only once, storing it temporarily in the
register
.Ar R .
.Pp
Another example is taken from the syntax built into the compiler:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<EXP> ::= <A> FOR <B> FROM <C> ::= LOCAL FOR IN "B:=C;
                              FOR: A;
                              (B:=B-1) GE 0=>GO TO FOR";
.Ed
.Pp
The list of local variables may consist of up to ten names, separated
by commas.
.Pp
This concludes the introduction to syntactic macros.
.Ss "2.3.2 Specifying Syntax"
The form of the syntax specification statement is a modification of
the form of a BNF production (Naur 1963), with alternative right-hand
sides not allowed, and a semantics definition added at the right.
The general form is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<class> ::= syntax-part ::= semantic-part
.Ed
.Pp
We will use the term
.Dq arguments
of a production or syntax statement to refer to the non-terminals appearing
in the syntax-part of a syntax statement (i.e., on the right-hand side
of the production).
.Pp
The compiler interprets a program by recognizing instances of certain
syntactic classes, such as
.Li <EXP>
(expression),
.Li <VBL>
(variable), and so on.
The function of the syntax part of a syntax statement is to tell the
compiler about a new construct that it must recognize as an instance
of a certain class, specified by the identifier in
.Li <class> .
The classes of interest in the IMP language are:
.Bl -tag -width 10n -compact
.It Li <NAM>
Any variable name or constant.
Should generally be avoided in favor of
.Li <VBL>
unless the user is sure of what he is doing.
.It Li <VBL>
Any constant or simple or subscripted variable.
.It Li <ION>
Any
.Li <VBL> ,
function call, or an
.Li <STL>
enclosed in parentheses.
.It Li <ATOM>
An
.Li <ION>
or byte of an
.Li <ION> .
.It Li <BYTE>
Any byte or byte pointer reference.
.It Li <EXP>
Class of most expressions.
.It Li <ST>
Includes
.Li <EXP>\(aqs ,
conditional expressions, declarations, etc.
.It Li <STL>
List of one or more
.Li <ST>s ,
separated by ;'s.
.El
.Pp
The full set of syntactic classes in IMP may be determined by reading
the syntax of the language (see Appendix II).
The user may define his own syntactic classes as the humor falls upon
him.
.Pp
The syntactic part of a syntax statement may contain:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
Identifiers and special characters, representing themselves.
The characters
.Li "%, \(dq, :, <"
and
.Li \&;
must be enclosed in single quotes.
.It 2.
Single characters enclosed in single quotes.
They are interpreted as the character alone.
.It 3.
Syntactic classes, represented by
.Li <CLASS,name> ,
where
.Ic CLASS
is the name of the class and name is an identifier by which to refer
to this argument of the production in the semantics.
.It 4.
.Li <name> .
This is interpreted as
.Li <EXP,name>
unless it is the very first thing after the
.Li ::= ,
in which case it is interpreted as
.Li <ATOM,name> .
This has the effect of making operations defined using
.Li <name>
be right-associative, in keeping with the IMP convention.
.El
.Pp
Example: In section 2.3.1, an expurgated version of the syntax of
.Ic FROM
loops was given.
The adult version illustrates the use of syntactic classes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<ST> ::= <EXP,A> FOR <VBL,B> FROM <EXP,C> ::=
                 LOCAL FOR IN "B:=C;
                              FOR: A;
                              (B:=B-1) GE 0=>GO TO FOR"
.Ed
.Pp
The interpretation of the syntactic part of this statement is that the
compiler is henceforth to recognize as an
.Li <ST>
the construct consisting of any
.Li <EXP> ,
followed by the word
.Ic FOR ,
followed by any
.Li <VBL> ,
followed by the word
.Ic FROM ,
followed by any
.Li <EXP> .
.Pp
Example: This example illustrates the use of user-defined syntactic
classes and recursive definitions.
The construct to be defined is
.Ic GO TO
.Pf \&( Li L0,L1,..,LN )
.Ar I
which transfers control to Li where i is the value of
.Ar I .
(If
.Li I<0
or
.Li I>N ,
the effect is undefined.)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<ST> ::= GO TO (<GOLIST,A>) <B>  ::=
           LOCAL GO IN "GO TO GO[B];GO: A";
<GOLIST> ::= <NAM,A>             ::= "GO TO A";
<GOLIST> ::= <GOLIST,A>,<NAM,B>  ::= "A; GO TO B"
Then the expression
GO TO (A,B,C) J-3
produces
GO TO %GO1[J-3];
%GO1: GO TO A;
     GO TO B;
     GO TO C
.Ed
.Pp
.Pf \&( Li %GO1
is a unique name generated by the compiler for the local variable
.Ic GO . )
This is the definition of the construct in the syntax file of the compiler.
The token
.Li :=
must be written in single quotes in the syntax part of a syntax statement,
as
.Ql := ,
like the other characters that the notation uses (the token
.Li ::=
that separates the parts needs none).
.Pp
It is possible to write a syntax statement with no semantic part:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<class> ::= syntax-part
.Ed
.Pp
The semantics implied by this syntax statement is to discard all arguments
of the production except the first.
If there are no arguments or one argument, no information is lost.
.Ss "2.3.3 Specifying Semantics"
.Ss "2.3.3.1 Semantic Routines"
In Section 2.3.1 we saw that the semantic part of a syntax statement
could consist of quoted semantics - i.e., an IMP expression enclosed
by double quotes.
There is one alternate format for semantics: a functional expression
consisting of calls to semantic subroutines.
.Pp
Semantic subroutines are normal compiler subroutines which are also
available to the syntax writer in a limited sense.
They are called with arguments of the following types:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
Constants.
Usually small positive numbers.
These can specify opcodes, switches, or whatever, depending on the routine.
.It 2.
Negative directory indices.
Identifiers are, by convention, passed to semantic routines as the negative
of their index in the directory.
.It 3.
Registers.
These are in the form of indices in a table of symbolic registers.
Assignments to actual machine registers are made during the assembly
phase.
.It 4.
Objects.
.It 5.
Two arguments connected by a
.Li +
sign.
The value is the sum of the arguments.
A syntactic ambiguity diagnostic will result from an argument of the
form
.Li arg+arg+arg ;
the diagnostic may be ignored.
.El
.Pp
Objects are pointers into a stack which holds the expressions being
processed by the semantics of the current production.
An object corresponds to some
.Li <class,name>
in the production an instance of which is being compiled.
Objects either are identifiers from the program being compiled, or have
been constructed by semantics operating on and/or combining other objects.
.Pp
Objects come in five flavors: Name, Register, Constant, Variable and
Memory: All but Name will have an arithmetic type (real or integer),
and may have associated with them some object code which computes the
expression of which the object is the value.
Name - Has no properties other than a name.
Is turned into another type of object by semantic routine
.Ic NAME
(q.v.).
Names will not pop up as operands; they will have been passed through
.Ic NAME
on their way to becoming
.Li <VBL>s .
.Pp
Register - Has no properties other than a register.
A Register object usually designates a register holding the result of
a computation.
.Pp
Constant - May be a constant from the source program (in which case
it has a name), or a computed one.
In any event, it has a value.
.Pp
Variable - Designates a simple variable, with maybe a constant subscript.
No other properties.
.Pp
Memory - Designates some address in memory too complicated to be a Variable.
May have any or all of a name, a constant subscript, an index register
(containing the value of a computed subscript), and even an indirect
bit.
.Ss "2.3.3.1.1 Calling Conventions for Semantic Routines"
The semantic part of a syntax statement may consist of a call to a semantic
routine, in standard IMP subroutine call format.
The arguments of the call may be either:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
Constants, unsigned, less than 30 bits.
The argument passed to the semantic routine is that number.
.It 2.
Identifiers appearing as names of instances of classes in the production
(e.g., A in the example of Section 2.3.1).
The argument passed to the semantic routine is a
.Dq stack pointer
to the object recognized as that part of the production.
.It 3.
Identifiers not appearing as names of instances of classes.
The argument passed to the semantic routine is the negative of the directory
index of the name.
.It 4.
Another call to a semantic routine.
The argument passed is the value of the semantic routine.
.It 5.
Any two of the above connected by a
.Li +
sign.
The argument passed is the indicated sum.
.El
.Pp
Although a semantic part consisting of calls on semantic routines looks
like part of an IMP program, there are certain conventions which the
semantics writer may take advantage of here.
Semantic routines may have up to 10 arguments.
Arguments are evaluated strictly left to right within one function call.
If more or fewer arguments are provided than the routine demands, no
difficulty is encountered (except that a reference to a missing argument
will refer to a random location in user core).
.Pp
Therefore, although the
.Dq Li \&;
operator is not provided in the syntax for semantic routine calls, its
effects often can be achieved through the use of arguments which will
be evaluated but not used.
For example, the effect of
.Bd -literal -offset indent
F(A,B); G(C,D,E)
.Ed
.Pp
may be obtained by
.Bd -literal -offset indent
G(C,D,E,F(A,B))
.Ed
.Pp
if it does not matter that in the latter case
.Ar C ,
.Ar D
and
.Ar E
are evaluated first.
.Pp
The following is an example of a syntax statement using semantic subroutines
to specify semantics.
It is suggested that the reader interpret the semantic routine calls
using the table below.
Operation 3 of the abstract machine is
.Ic COMPL ,
the ones complement.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<ATOM>::= INV ( <ATOM,A> ) ::= DEWOP(3,AREG1(1,15B),A);
.Ed
.Pp
After this statement has been compiled (in a file of syntax, Section
3.4), the expression
.Li INV(5\&)
has the value -6.
.Ss "2.3.3.1.2 Table of Semantic Routines"
In this table, the types of arguments expected are indicated as follows:
.Ar S ,
.Ar T ,
.Ar U :
Objects (other than Names except where specified).
.Pp
.Li *
Indicates a routine which is object machine-dependent (i.e., which must
be altered if code for another computer is to be generated.)
.Ic NAME AND ARGUMENTS EFFECT AND RESULT RETURNED .
.Bl -tag -width 18n -compact
.It Li ADDOP(I,S,T\&)
.Li *
Performs a binary operation on
.Ar S
and
.Ar T .
It is defined as
.Li "HOOK(S,T, DEWOP(I,REGOF(FETCH(T)),S)\&)" .
This could be written in semantics but using
.Ic ADDOP
saves compiler table space and is clearer besides.
.It Li ADDR(S\&)
.Li *
Result is
.Ar S .
The operand of an operation of the abstract machine may be a constant,
so that no object with the value in the address field is needed.
.It Li AREG(I\&)
Returns the register index for hardware register
.Ar I .
.It Li AREG1(I,J\&)
Returns a brand-new register, a temporary of the abstract machine, of
which there is an unlimited supply.
.It Li BYTEP(S,T,U\&)
.Li *
.Ar S
is a Variable-type object.
Makes a byte pointer for
.Li S<T,U> ,
computed when the code runs (Section 2.2.6).
Result is
.Ar S ,
whose value is now the byte pointer.
Good things to
.Ic DEWOP
on
.Ar S
are the byte operations 24 to 27.
.It Li CONOP(S,T,I,U\&)
Performs an operation designated by I on two Constant-type objects
.Ar S
and
.Ar T .
See file
.Ic SYNTAX
or the source for
.Ic CONOP
for the codes for
.Ar I .
Feel free to add a few more operations if it will generate better code.
.Ar U
is the implicand in cases of
.Ic CON RELOP
.Li CON=>U .
Result is
.Ar S ,
with code for
.Ar T
hooked in first if there is any.
.It Li COPY(S\&)
Produces a copy of the object
.Ar S ,
including a copy of the code associated with it, if any.
Useful if
.Ar S
is to be hooked in in two different places in code to be generated.
Care must be taken to use the copy of
.Ar S
in the place which is hooked in first, since trying to copy an object
that has already been hooked in may imperil the internal tranquility
of the compiler.
.It Li DATAST(S\&)
For use in
.Ic DATA
statements.
.Ar S
is any old object; it gets clobbered.
Unpacks the current list (see
.Ic ENLIST ) ,
and generates code for the
.Ic DATA
statement whose constants and variables are in the list.
.It Ic DECL
.It Ic DECLARE
Special semantic routines to implement declarations.
.It Li DEWFUN(S,I,J\&)
Adds the code to the object
.Ar S
which instructs the assembly pass to perform special function
.Ar I ,
with argument
.Ar J .
.It Li DEWOP(I,R,S\&)
.Li *
Performs operation I of the abstract machine (the table below) upon
object
.Ar S
and (if nonzero) register
.Ar R .
This is a very smart routine and is happy with any object (except a
Name) as
.Ar S .
Caution: If
.Ic DEWOP
is used to implement a binary operator (as in
.Li DEWOP(OP,REGOF(T),S\&) ,
the code for
.Ar T
must be hooked on to the code for
.Ar S
after the
.Ic DEWOP
is performed, otherwise it will be lost.
See
.Ic ADDOP .
Result of
.Ic DEWOP
is
.Ar S .
If
.Ar R
is nonzero,
.Ar S
is now a Register object, with value in the register designated by
.Ar R .
.It Li ENLIST(S\&)
Places the object
.Ar S
at the bottom of the current list.
.Ic ENLIST
is part of a general recursive list mechanism for stacking up lists
of things to be fed all at once to some semantic routine.
.Li NEWLIST(S\&)
creates an empty list, and, if
.Ar S
is nonzero, puts the object
.Ar S
on it.
.Li GETLIST(S\&)
is a non-semantic subroutine which unpacks the current list on a first-in-first-out
basis.
If the list is not empty,
.Li GETLIST(S\&)
puts the top object into the object
.Ar S
(clobbering the old value), and returns a non-zero value.
If the list is empty,
.Ic GETLIST
returns 0, and reopens the list which was current at the last call to
.Ic NEWLIST .
See
.Ic DECLARE
(file IMPSEM) for an example of a semantic routine which uses
.Ic GETLIST .
The result of
.Ic ENLIST
is 0.
.It Li ENSTACK(I\&)
Creates an object of type Name, where
.Ar I
is the directory index (either positive or negative) of the name.
For example to make the object corresponding to the variable
.Ic VAR ,
write
.Li NAME(ENSTACK(VAR)\&) .
.It Li ERROR(N,V\&)
Produces the compiler error message which is the name of the variable
.Ar V .
.Ar N
is 0, 1 or 2 for a fatal, ordinary, or advisory error respectively.
For error messages containing spaces, etc., enclose the message in !
signs to make it into a variable name.
Example:
.Li "ERROR(2,!You Goofed.!\&)" .
.It Li FETCH(S\&)
.Li *
Forces the object
.Ar S
to be of type Register (i.e., loads it into a register).
Result is
.Ar S .
.It Li FIX(S\&)
.Li *
Generates code to convert
.Ar S
from floating point to integer.
Value is
.Ar S .
.It Li FLOAT(S\&)
.Li *
Generates code to convert
.Ar S
from integer to floating point.
Value is
.Ar S .
.It Li HOOK(U,S,T\&)
Hooks the code for
.Ar T
on after the code for
.Ar S .
.Ar U
gets this code, and the value of
.Ar T .
.Ar U
may be one of
.Ar S
or
.Ar T
(and usually is).
.Ic HOOK
thus performs the
.Dq Li \&;
operator, among its other uses.
It must be used whenever two or more arguments which might have code
attached appear in a production, except in the few cases where another
semantic routine invokes
.Ic HOOK
implicitly.
The result of
.Ic HOOK
is
.Ar U .
.It Li MAXWELL
.It Li MAXEND
Specialized subroutines to handle the job of switching parser output
into a temporary array rather than feeding it to the code generator.
Used to implement quoted semantics.
.It Li NAME(S\&)
.Ar S
is a Name-type object.
.Ic NAME
turns it into a variable, adding an arithmetic type, and making things
right if
.Ar S
is a register or subroutine parameter.
Result is
.Ar S .
.It Li NEWLIST(S\&)
See
.Ic ENLIST .
.It Li OJUMPOP(I\&)
.Li *
.Ar I
is the code of a relational operator (1
.Li < ,
2
.Li = ,
3
.Ic LE ,
5
.Ic GE ,
6
.Ic NE ,
7
.Li > ) .
.Ic OJUMPOP
returns the code of the negation of that operator.
If
.Ar I
has the
.Li 10B
bit set,
.Ic OJUMPOP
returns the code for the reverse negation (the reverse of
.Li > ,
for example, is
.Li < . )
.It Li PAR(I\&)
Used in
.Ic CASE
semantics to refer to the Ith argument (counting from 0) of the
.Ic CASE
argument list (see section 2.3.3.3.).
.It Ic PRINCAL
.It Ic PRINPAR
.Li *
Specialized routines handling semantics for
.Ic PRINT
and
.Ic READ PRINPAR
statements.
.It Li REGOF(S\&)
Result is the register of the object
.Ar S ,
or 0 if it doesn’t have one.
.It Li REG2S(S\&)
If
.Ar S
is of type register, and the register was assigned by
.Li AREG1
so as to reserve two consecutive registers, then the value of
.Ar S
becomes the contents of the second register.
.Ar S
is otherwise unchanged.
If
.Ar S
is not as specified, undefined things happen.
The value of REG2S is
.Ar S .
.It Li REMOTE(S\&)
Causes the code associated with
.Ar S
to be inserted at the end of the compilation instead of at the current
point.
Result is
.Ar S ,
but with no code.
(This may lead to incorrect results if an attempt is made to compute
with the value of
.Ar S . )
.It Li REPVAL(S,T\&)
Part of the semantics for syntax.
Generalizes the
.Ic VALUE
class
.Ar S
within the quoted semantics
.Ar T
(see Section 2.3.3.4).
.It Li RETURN(S\&)
.Li *
Generates code to return from the current subroutine with
.Ar S
as the value of the subroutine.
Result is
.Ar S ,
not that it matters.
.It Li STACKUP(I\&)
.Ar I
is either a negative directory index or a constant.
This routine is used to create objects and place them on the stack.
(If
.Ar I
is a constant, the constant is first entered in the directory, so that
a directory index for the item exists.)
.Li NAME(ENSTACK(I)\&)
is then performed to make the object.
Result is stack index of the item.
.It Li SETPRI(I\&)
Part of the semantics for syntax.
Sets the designated priority bits in the semantics being generated.
.It Li STACK(I\&)
Returns the Ith argument of the production.
Used to refer to arguments which are
.Ic VALUE
classes in default semantics (i.e., semantics which are not conditional.)
.It Li STORE(S,T\&)
.Li *
Stores the value of
.Ar T
in
.Ar S ,
hooking the code for
.Ar S
on after that for
.Ar T .
Result is
.Ar T .
.It Li SUBBEG(S\&)
.Li *
Generates code for the beginning of subroutine
.Ar S ,
where
.Ar S
is a Name-type object.
Result is
.Ar S ,
with the code attached.
.It Li SUBPR0(I\&)
Called with
.Li I=0
at the beginning, and
.Li I=1
at the end, of every subroutine call.
Keeps track of the level of calls, and resets the index of the next
temporary for subroutine arguments to 0 whenever it gets up to level
0.
.It Li SUBRCALL(S\&)
.Li *
.Ar S
must be of type Variable.
Generates a subroutine call to
.Ar S
(the operation
.Ic CALLB ) ,
and a reference to the function
.Ar S .
The call is completed by a
.Li DEWFUN(T,2,REGOF(T),SUBPR0(1)\&) ,
where
.Ar T
is the complete subroutine call with arguments.
The result of
.Ic SUBRCALL
is
.Ar S ,
whose value is now register
.Li 0R ,
which is where subroutines return values.
.It Li SUBRPAR(S,T\&)
.Li *
.Ar S
is a subroutine call or some similar thing being built, and
.Ar T
is an argument.
The address of
.Ar T
is passed as the next argument of the call (the operation
.Ic ARG ) .
If
.Ar T
has no address, the address is that of a temporary containing the value
of
.Ar T .
The code for
.Ar T
is hooked on to the beginning of
.Ar S .
The result is
.Ar S .
.It Li SUBSCRIPT(S,T\&)
.Li *
Generates code for the Variable- or Memory-type object
.Li S[T\&] .
Is smart about not computing constant subscripts, and so on.
.Ar S
and
.Ar T
may be any type except name.
Result is
.Ar S ,
with the code for
.Ar T
hooked on before.
.It Li SVAL(I\&)
Creates an object whose
.Ic VAL
is
.Ar I .
(See also
.Ic VAL ) .
.It Li SWITCH(I\&)
Turns on the compiler switch which is the Ith letter of the alphabet.
.It Li TAG(S\&)
.Ar S
had better be an object of type Name, or Variable without a subscript.
Adds to
.Ar S
the code which defines the tag
.Ar S
at that point.
Result is
.Ar S .
.It Li VAL(S\&)
Returns the low order 32 bits of the value of the object
.Ar S .
If
.Ar S
is a constant, then this is the low 32 bits of its value.
If
.Ar S
is the result of a
.Ic VALUE
semantics, then this is the value specified there.
If
.Ar S
has had a value set by
.Ic SVAL ,
then this is that value.
.It Li VALU(S,T\&)
Part of the semantics for syntax.
Defines the current semantics to be
.Ic VALUE
.Ar S
.Ic OF
.Ar T ,
where
.Ar S
is a Constant object and
.Ar T
is a Name object.
.El
.Bl -tag -width 18n -compact
.It Li MOVEBLK(S,N,T\&)
Generates the call of the library routine that copies the
.Li N+1
words from
.Ar S
to
.Ar T ;
the semantics of
.Ic MOVE .
.It Li JOP(C\&)
The jump operation (41 to 46) for the relational operator with code
.Ar C ;
10 more for a comparison of reals.
.It Li CJUMP(OP,P,R,S\&)
Jump to the tag
.Ar S
if
.Ar P
and
.Ar R
are related by the jump operation
.Ic OP .
.It Li CVAL(S\&)
Makes the name
.Ar S
the name of a
.Ar C
function that is called with its arguments by value (Section 2.4.1);
the semantics of
.Ic CCALL
use it.
.It Li CDECL(T,F,L,V\&)
Enters the types of the
.Ar C
function
.Ar F ,
of the result type
.Ar T
and the parameter types in the list
.Ar L
(0 for none);
.Ar V
is the sum of 1 if the function takes more arguments, 2 for a callback
and 4 for a call through a pointer (Section 2.4.1, 2.4.5).
.Li CSUBCALL(S,L\&)
and
.Li CCALL0(S\&)
call it, checking the number of the arguments and converting them to
the declared types.
.It Li CBADDR(A\&)
Makes the address of the
.Ar C
function that calls the
.Ic SUBR
named
.Ar A ,
which
.Ic CDECL
declared a callback
.Li "(V is 2\&)"
(Section 2.4.5).
For a pointer call
.Ar V
is 4, or 5 with the dots.
.It Li STBEG(N\&)
Begins the declaration of the structure
.Li N
(section 2.4.6);
.Li STFLD(T,F,N\&)
adds the field
.Li F
of the type
.Li T
(an array of
.Li N
if
.Li N
is not 0) and places it by the alignment rules of the target, and
.Li STEND(H\&)
ends the structure and rounds its size.
.It Li STSIZE(S\&)
Makes the constant that is the size of the structure or type of
.Ar C
named
.Li "S; FPBEG(S,F\&)"
and
.Li FPFLD(P,F\&)
follow a path of fields from a structure, and
.Li STOFF(P\&)
makes the constant that is the offset the path ends at.
.It Li FLDP(P,F,I\&)
Makes the descriptor of the field at the end of the path
.Li F
of the structure at the address
.Li P ,
with the element
.Li I
(0 for none);
.Li DEWOP(24,R,D\&)
loads it and
.Li FLDST(A,B\&)
converts
.Li B
to its type and stores it, with the operation 25.
.It Li FCON(A,B,C\&)
Makes the floating point constant A.B"C.
.Pp
In addition to the above, there are a number of semantic routines which
define the semantics for syntax, all on file RSYN.
See Section 4.4 for documentation.
.Pp
The operations of the abstract machine are the following.
The accumulator
.Ic RD
is a register and
.Ar V
the operand.
Integers are 64-bit two’s complement numbers;
.Ic DIV
truncates toward zero and
.Ic MOD
has the sign of the dividend.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
1 LOAD  RD=V      2 NEG  RD=-V      3 COMPL  RD=~V      4 ADDR  RD=the address of V
5 STORE V=RD
6 ADD  7 SUB  8 MUL  9 DIV  10 MOD                       RD=RD op V
11 AND  12 ANDCA (~RD & V)  13 ANDCM (RD & ~V)  14 ANDCB (~RD & ~V)
15 IOR  16 ORCA (~RD | V)  17 ORCM (RD | ~V)  18 ORCB (~RD | ~V)
19 XOR  20 EQV (~(RD ^ V))
21 LSH  RD shifted logically by the signed count V (left if positive);
        64 places or more give 0
22 ASH  the same, arithmetic    23 ROT  RD rotated left by V
24 LDB  RD=the byte field V names    25 DPB  the low bits of RD into the field V names
26 ILDB 27 IDPB  the same after stepping the byte pointer V
28 ADDX  RD=RD+8*V (a word index added to a pointer)
30 FADD  31 FSUB  32 FMUL  33 FDIV                      RD=RD op V, on doubles
34 FLOAT RD=(double)V    35 FIX  RD=(long)V, truncated    36 FNEG  RD=-V
40 JUMP  to the tag named by V
41 JEQ  42 JLT  43 JLE  44 JGE  45 JGT  46 JNE         jump to the tag if RD cmp V
51 to 56 FJEQ to FJNE                                   the same on doubles
60 CALLB begin a call    61 ARG  the address of V is the next argument
62 CALLE end the call, RD receives the value
63 PITEM print list item: the format code, and its operand
64 CALLI  RD=the library routine IMPCALLI(V,RD)    65 XCT  do the instruction in the word V
66 HRLM  67 HRRM  the low 32 bits of RD into the left or right half of V
.Ed
.El
.Pp
The operand
.Ar V
is a register, a constant, a variable, or a subscripted variable (a
variable plus 8 times a word offset, plus 8 times a register).
An address is a byte address.
.Ss "2.3.3.2 Conditional Semantics"
We saw above that semantics may consist of either a semantic routine
call or quoted semantics.
It is possible to invoke one of a number of semantics for a given syntactic
production, depending on the particular case of that production being
compiled.
First an example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<ATOM>::= NOT <ATOM,A>  ::= "NOT B0CON"=>CONOP(B,0,6) ELSE
DEWOP(3,AREG1(1,15B),A);
.Ed
.Pp
The semantic part of this syntax statement invokes different semantics
for the special case of NOT-constant.
.Pp
Semantics may consist of a number of alternatives, separated by the
identifier
.Ic ELSE .
One alternative may consist simply of a semantic routine call or quoted
semantics: this is the semantics executed if none of the special cases
obtain.
The other alternatives are of the form
.Bd -literal -offset indent
condition => semantics
.Ed
.Pp
where the semantics is a semantic routine call or quoted semantics,
and the condition is an IMP expression which is a case of the production,
enclosed in double quotes.
The arguments of the semantics should be identifiers which designate
arguments in the condition.
Referring to identifiers which designate arguments in the syntax part
will not give a diagnostic but might produce undesired results.
.Pp
The condition is satisfied if the expression being parsed matches the
expression in the condition.
Constants in the condition will match any constant with the same value,
even if the names are different.
(But constant expressions in the condition are not evaluated.
Thus,
.Dq -18
in a condition would only match in cases where a minus sign was actually
used before a constant with value 18.
One should trust the compiler to perform constant arithmetic, and write
.Li 1777777777777777777756B
instead, which will match any constant expression which evaluates to
-18.) Variable names will match any
.Li <EXP> ,
unless they are tagged with modifiers.
If the same name appears twice or more in the condition, the expressions
matching all occurrences must be identical.
.Pp
Modifiers may be added to the first occurrence of a given variable name
in a condition by adding the character
.Ql 0
to the name, followed by the modifiers.
Modifiers all consist of three letters, sometimes followed by one or
two two-digit octal numbers.
For example, A0CON, FOO0REG0115, I0IGRVARMEMREG.
The modifiers are:
Arithmetic type modifiers (Identifiers with neither of these match either
type):
.Pp
.Ic IGR :
Identifier matches only objects of type integer.
.Pp
Object type modifiers (Identifiers with none of these match any object
type; identifiers with several match any of the indicated types):
.Pp
.Ic REG :
Matches objects of type Register.
.Pp
Code modifier:
.Pp
.Ic WRD :
Matches objects with exactly one word of code associated with them.
May not always succeed when one might think it should, due to the order
in which objects are combined by the code generator, but will always
fail when it should.
.Pp
Numerous examples of conditional semantics may be found on file
.Ic SYNTAX ,
which contains the syntax for IMP72.
.Pp
Care should be taken to specify special cases as cases of the proper
production.
For example, suppose one wished to refer to the low 32 bits (the right
half) of a variable by the construct
.Li /VARIABLE/ .
This might be accomplished by
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<VBL> ::= / <VBL,A> / ::= "A<32,0>"
.Ed
.Pp
which names the byte of A that is its right half.
If one wanted also to be able to write
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/VARIABLE/ := EXPRESSION
.Ed
.Pp
and store the value of the expression in the right half of variable,
one might then write
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<VBL> ::= / <VBL,A> / ::= "A<32,0>"
   ELSE "/A/:=B" => "A<32,0>:=B"
.Ed
.Pp
But
.Li /A/:=B
is a special case not of this production but of the production for
.Dq Li := .
The above syntax statement would produce an error diagnostic.
The correct definition would be (the token
.Li :=
is written in quotes in the syntax part of a syntax statement, like
the other characters that the notation uses)
.Pp
.Li "<vbl> ::= / <vbl,a> / ::="
.Dq Li a<32,0> ;
.Li "<exp> ::= <vbl,a>"
.Ql :=
.Li "<exp,b> ::="
.Dq Li /a/:=b
.Li =>
.Dq Li a<32,0>:=b
.Pp
This would produce an advisory diagnostic, since the syntax in the second
statement duplicates a production already in the language, but this
is permitted in order to allow defining additional special cases of
semantics, exactly as is done here.
.Ss "2.3.3.3 CASEs"
If two or more productions have exactly the same syntax, except for
terminal symbols (i.e., they contain the same syntactic classes in the
same order, and are instances of the same class), and if their semantics
are defined by identical semantic routine calls, with perhaps only a
few constants changed (e.g., different opcodes), then it is possible
to define a general case, and define the semantics of each production
as a special case of it.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<EXP> ::= <ATOM,I> LS <EXP,J> ::= CASE (21,0,0,5) OF
      SHIFTS  (ADDOP(PAR(0),J,I)
      ELSE "A0CON LS B0CON"=>CONOP(A,B,PAR(3)));
<EXP> ::= <ATOM,A>  ALS <EXP,B> ::=
       CASE (22,0,0,16) OF SHIFTS;
<EXP> ::= <ATOM,A> LROT <EXP,B> ::=
       CASE (23,0,0,17) OF SHIFTS;
.Ed
.Pp
The first statement defines the syntax for
.Ar I
.Ic LS
.Ar J .
The semantic part defines the case
.Ic SHIFTS
to consist of the set of semantic alternatives within the parentheses
following the identifier
.Ic SHIFTS .
(Notice that the A0CON
.Ic LS
B0CON alternative is not part of the
.Ic SHIFTS
definition but is an alternative to it.) The list of constants following
the identifier
.Ic CASE
is the
.Dq parameter list
for this particular instance
.Pf \&( Ic LS )
of the case
.Ic SHIFTS .
These constants are referred to in the semantics for
.Ic SHIFTS
by
.Li PAR(0\&)
and
.Li PAR(3\&) ;
the first is the operation of the abstract machine (21 is
.Ic LSH )
and the fourth is the code of the operation for
.Ic CONOP .
.Pp
The other two statements define the syntax and semantics for two other
kinds of shifts.
The semantics is defined by invoking
.Ic SHIFTS
with different parameter lists.
The semantics are the same, but
.Li PAR(i\&)
will refer to the i-th element of the appropriate parameter list.
.Pp
A case, such as
.Ic SHIFTS ,
may not be defined in more than one place.
It is not necessary to have parameter lists be the same length for different
instances of a case, but it is the user’s responsibility to insure that
parameters that are not supplied are not referred to.
In any event, parameter lists must always contain at least one constant.
.Pp
Several
.Ic CASE
semantics may appear as some or all of the alternatives in a semantic
part.
A
.Ic CASE
semantics may not be the semantics part of a conditional semantics alternative
(i.e.,
.Pf \(dq Li condition\(dq=>CASE
(...)
.Ic OF FOO
is illegal.)
.Ss "2.3.3.4 The VALUE Kludge"
Occasionally, it is necessary to refer in conditional semantics to an
argument of the production which can not be written as an identifier.
This is not possible using the mechanisms defined so far.
The
.Ic VALUE
kludge is a way to accomplish this.
Take for example the syntax for conditionals.
Relational operators are defined as follows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<RELOP> ::= NE  ::= VALUE 6 OF EQ;
<RELOP> ::= '<' ::= VALUE 1 OF EQ;
.Ed
.Pp
and so on.
The semantics specifies that these productions are special cases of
a class of productions labeled
.Ic EQ ,
and gives a 32-bit value to be associated with the particular production.
.Pp
In syntax statements involving the class
.Li <RELOP> ,
it is possible to specify that any case of
.Ic EQ
may be recognized as matching a particular element in a semantic condition.
This is done as follows:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
In quoted unconditional semantics: prefix the quoted part by
.Li EQ/ .
The corresponding instance of
.Ic RELOP
in the syntax part must be named
.Ic EQ .
(Last line in the example below).
.It 2.
In semantic conditions: Prefix the condition by
.Li EQ/ .
.It 3.
In semantics: The element may be referred to in quoted semantics by
prefixing the quoted part by
.Li EQ/
as in (1).
In semantic routine calls, the element may be referenced by
.Li STACK(i\&) ,
where the element comes to the right of exactly i identifiers in the
condition (or, in unconditional semantics, i arguments of the production).
.El
.Pp
The particular instance of
.Ic EQ
may be determined by referring to
.Li VAL(STACK(i)\&) ,
with i as in (2) above.
The value of this semantic routine call will be the value specified
in the
.Ic VALUE
semantics defining the instance of
.Li <RELOP>
in the expression being compiled.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<ST>    ::= <EXP,A> <RELOP,EQ> <EXP,B> => <ST,C> ::=
  EQ/"P=R=>GO TO S0VAR"=>CJUMP(JOP(VAL(STACK(1))),P,R,S) ELSE
  LOCAL IF IN EQ/"NOT(A=B)=>GO TO IF; C; IF: 0";
.Ed
.Ss "2.3.3.5 Priority Semantics"
Usually, the semantics for an expression is not executed until enough
of the context of the expression has been parsed in order to determine
the particular special case involved.
If it is known that no special cases involving subexpressions of a production
exist, or if it is desired for other reasons to defeat the special case
matching mechanism and invoke the semantics for a production immediately
it is recognized, the
.Ic PRIORITY
semantics is used.
.Pp
A
.Ic PRIORITY
semantics is a semantic part consisting entirely of
.Bd -literal -offset indent
PRIORITY n s
.Ed
.Pp
where n is a digit in the range 0-7, and s is a semantic part containing
no conditional semantics or
.Ic ELSE
alternatives.
.Pp
n is interpreted as a three-bit mask, and the semantics s for the production
is executed immediately, bypassing the special case matching process,
in the event that the current priority mask of the matching routine
and n have any bits set in the same position.
.Pp
The priority mask of the matching routine is set to k by the semantic
routine
.Li SETPRI(k\&) .
The interpretations of the bits are:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
Normally set.
Used to force immediate semantic execution under normal circumstances.
.It 2.
Set during interpretation of double-quoted expressions.
In this mode, semantics are not interpreted unless they are
.Ic PRIORITY
2.
The output of the parser is stored instead, for use as data by the compiler.
However,
.Ic PRIORITY
2 semantics are executed, providing a way to terminate this state.
.It 4.
Set during synonym processing
.Li "(LET statement\&)" .
.El
.Ss "2.3.4 Syntactic Ambiguity and How to Make it Work for You"
The IMP72 parser allows ambiguity in the syntax.
For example, notice that in many examples above, we present different
definitions of the construct
.Li ABS(E\&) .
The syntax allows two interpretations of this construct: the special
construct we are defining, or a call on the subroutine
.Ic ABS .
The parser notes the two interpretations, and chooses between them,
as follows:
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
If an identifier is an expression in one interpretation, and a terminal
symbol (i.e., appears as itself in the production, such as
.Ic ABS
in the example of Section 2.3.1), in the other interpretation, the interpretation
with the terminal symbol is chosen.
.It 2.
Otherwise, the interpretation involving the smallest number of different
syntax rules is chosen.
.It 3.
Otherwise, the compiler makes an arbitrary choice.
.El
.Pp
Thus, referring to the example in Section 2.3.3.2 of the construct /variable/,
the special case
.Li /variable/:=expression
was defined by a special case in the semantics.
It would alternatively be possible to define the case by introducing
a deliberate syntactic ambiguity, as follows:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<VBL> ::= / <VBL,A> /
                    ::= "A<32,0>";
<EXP>  ::= / <VBL,A> / ':=' <EXP,B>
                    ::= "A<32,0>:=B"
.Ed
.Pp
The expression
.Li /X/:=5 ,
e.g., could be recognized in two ways, by using the first syntax statement
above plus the rule for
.Li <VAR>:=<EXP> ,
or by using the second syntax statement above.
But according to rule 2, the parser chooses the second interpretation,
as involving one syntax rule as opposed to two rules for the first interpretation.
This is precisely the choice that is desired.
.Ss "2.3.5 Peaceful Co-Existence with Your Extensible Compiler"
This section presents the results of some experience with syntax and
semantic definition in IMP72, in order to help others avoid some problems.
.Pp
The parser works by carrying along all possible parses of the program
up to the symbol it is reading at the moment.
It usually manages to resolve all ambiguity every couple of symbols
at most.
If a syntactic production is entered which requires it to read a large
number of symbols before it can choose which syntax rule it is following,
the parser may require inordinate amounts of space and time.
This condition may be diagnosed by looking at the compiler statistics
at the bottom of the source program listing.
If the Max.
Parse Space goes over
.Li 1500+500 ,
or the Max.
Output Space is over 150, the condition may exist.
.Pp
An example of syntax which may cause the problem is:
.Pp
.Li "<exp> ::= <exp,a> => <st,b>; <exp> ::= <exp,a> => <exp,b>"
else
.Li <st,c>
.Pp
When the compiler sees
.Li A=>( . . . . . . . ) ,
it is not able to decide until the right parenthesis whether it is trying
to form an
.Ic ST
or an
.Ic EXP .
Thus it must carry along two parses while it is parsing the entire contents
of the parentheses, which may be an arbitrarily long expression.
The syntax IMP uses is
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<EXP> ::= <EXP,A> => <ST,B>;
<EXP> ::= <EXP,A> => <ST,B> ELSE <ST,C>
.Ed
.Pp
which avoids the parser problem (although it introduces the necessity
for parenthesizing the
.Ic ELSE
clause).
The method for storing syntax rules combines all rules whose right-hand
parts begin identically, up to the point at which they differ.
Thus, the parser stores these two rules as:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
                  =:: <EXP>
                 /
<EXP,A> => <ST,B>
                 \e
                  ELSE <ST,C> =:: <EXP>
.Ed
.Pp
and only carries along one parse until it has finished parsing the
.Li <ST,B> .
.Pp
Another difficulty which may arise is that special semantic cases may
be specified in a syntax statement, but the compiler will refuse to
recognize them.
This may arise from the way in which the patterns for special cases
are recognized.
Semantics are performed for subexpressions of a syntax production from
left to right.
For an element in a syntactic conditional to be recognized, it must
already have been reduced by semantics to one object.
An example will illustrate the pitfall:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<EXP> ::= <ATOM,A> NE <EXP,B> ::= .....;
<ST>  ::= <EXP,A> => <ST,B> ::=
       "A NE 0=>B" => ..... ELSE .....
.Ed
.Pp
where the \&..... represents semantics whose exact form is nonessential
to the point being illustrated.
If the statement
.Bd -literal -offset indent
A NE 0=>X:=X+4
.Ed
.Pp
is encountered by the compiler, an attempt to match the special case
.Dq Li "A NE 0=>B"
is made, but fails since
.Li X:=X+4
is not a single object; the semantics for
.Li :=
and
.Li +
have not yet been performed.
Since the special case fails, the semantics for the subexpressions of
the statement are invoked, from left to right.
First, the semantics for
.Ar A
.Ic NE
.Ar B
is performed.
Checks are made at every step to see if the current expression matches
some special case, but now
.Ar A
.Ic NE
0 is a single object, and will only match patterns of the form
.Dq Li X=>..\&. .
Thus the compiler will fail to recognize the special case
.Dq Li "A NE 0=>B" .
.Pp
It is in order to avoid this problem that IMP has separate productions
for
.Li A=>B
and
.Ar A
.Li "<RELOP> B=>C"
(where
.Li <RELOP>
is the class of relational operators).
.Ss "2.4 Calling C and Being Called from C"
An IMP routine is a function of the
.Li C
calling convention of the system, and a
.Li C
function can be called from IMP.
The files are in the object format of the system (ELF or Mach-O), so
that they link with
.Ar C
objects and libraries.
The runtime library of IMP programs,
.Li libimp.a ,
must be linked with them.
.Ss "2.4.1 Calling C: CCALL"
.Li "CCALL NAME(A,B,...\&)"
calls the
.Li C
function
.Li NAME
passing the values of its arguments, as
.Ar C
does, and has the value that the function returns.
An ordinary call
.Li NAME(A,B\&)
of an IMP routine passes addresses; a call of a
.Ar C
function is always written with
.Ic CCALL .
The arguments go in the registers and on the stack that the
.Li C
ABI of the system uses, so that a call may have more than six (sixteen
at most).
A function that is not declared (see below) is called with a fixed number
of arguments, each one word (type
.Li long ) :
the arguments are integers or pointers, and the result is the integer
of a pointer’s size.
A
.Ar C
function that takes a variable number of arguments
.Li "(printf, snprintf, open, fcntl, ioctl, syscall\&)"
must be declared with
.Li \&... ,
because the ABI of several systems calls a variadic function differently
from a fixed one.
The address of a variable or of a string constant, which a
.Li C
function expects for a pointer, is written
.Li LOC(X\&)
or LOC('text'): a string constant is bytes ended by a zero byte.
For example
.Bd -literal -offset indent
N:=CCALL strlen(LOC('hello, world'));
PRINT STG 0,'length ',IGR 0,N,/;
CCALL puts(LOC('puts adds a newline'));
.Ed
.Pp
call
.Li strlen
and
.Li puts .
(after
.Li "EXTERNAL INT printf(PTR, ...);, CCALL printf(LOC(\(aq%ld\(aq),N\&)"
prints with the formats of
.Ar C . )
A
.Ar C
.Li int
result of a function that is not declared comes back in half a register,
so that its upper bits are undefined: mask it with
.Li "AND 4294967295"
before comparing it with a negative number.
A function that is not declared cannot take or return a
.Li double
or a
.Li float .
A declaration removes these limits.
.Pp
A declaration gives the types of a
.Ar C
function once, as a statement:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
EXTERNAL INT puts(PTR);
EXTERNAL DOUBLE ldexp(DOUBLE, INT);
EXTERNAL PTR malloc(SIZE);
EXTERNAL VOID free(PTR);
EXTERNAL INT printf(PTR, ...);
EXTERNAL LONG write(INT, PTR, SIZE);
.Ed
.Pp
The types are those of
.Ar C ,
written in capitals (names are case sensitive):
.Li "CHAR UCHAR SHORT USHORT INT UINT LONG ULONG LLONG ULLONG SIZE PTR DOUBLE FLOAT" ,
and
.Li VOID
for a result or as the whole list of parameters
.Li "((VOID) is no parameter\&)" .
.Li "LONG, ULONG"
and
.Li SIZE
have the width of a
.Ar C
.Li long ,
which is 32 bits where pointers are 32 bits;
.Li LLONG
and
.Li ULLONG
are 64 bits everywhere, and
.Li PTR
is the width of a pointer.
The name is the
.Ar C
name as written; a name that is not an IMP name is a special identifier
(Section 2.2.1).
Three dots after the last type declare a function of a variable number
of arguments.
A declaration holds to the end of the file, and a function that several
files call is declared in each.
A function declared twice with different types, declared after a call
of it that had no declaration, or declared with a type that is not one
of those, is an error.
.Pp
In a call
.Li "CCALL NAME(ARGS\&)"
of a declared function the compiler counts the arguments (a wrong number
is an error, and a variadic function takes at least the declared ones)
and converts each as
.Ar C
does.
An integer is truncated or extended to the type of the parameter.
.Ar A
.Li PTR
takes a word as its value, or a string constant directly:
.Li "CCALL puts(\(aqhello\(aq\&)"
passes the address of the bytes, ended by a zero byte, with no
.Ic LOC .
.Ar A
.Li DOUBLE
or a
.Li FLOAT
takes a
.Ic REAL ,
which is the bits of a double (Section 2.4.3), or an integer, which
is converted to real; a
.Ic REAL
given to an integer type or a
.Li PTR
is converted to an integer.
The arguments of a variadic call that have no declared type get the
promotions of
.Ar C :
a
.Ic REAL
is passed as a
.Li double ,
and an integer as a
.Li long ;
the address of a string constant there is written LOC('text').
The result is the word the program sees: the signed types are sign-extended,
the unsigned types and
.Li PTR
are zero-extended, a
.Li DOUBLE
or a
.Li FLOAT
is a
.Ic REAL ,
and
.Li VOID
gives 0.
A function declared
.Li DOUBLE
or
.Li FLOAT
has a
.Ic REAL
as its value, which mixes with other reals; a variable or a function
that holds one is declared
.Ic REAL .
For example
.Bd -literal -offset indent
EXTERNAL INT puts(PTR);
EXTERNAL INT printf(PTR, ...);
EXTERNAL DOUBLE ldexp(DOUBLE, INT);
X IS REAL;
CCALL puts('ldexp scales by a power of two');
X:=CCALL ldexp(1.5,4);
CCALL printf('%.1f%c',X,10);
FINI(0)
%%
.Ed
.Pp
prints
.Li "ldexp scales by a power of two"
and
.Li 24.0 .
.Li "CCALL puts(\(aqa\(aq,\(aqb\(aq\&)"
is an error:
.Li "CCALL puts TAKES 1 ARGUMENTS, NOT 2\&."
The compiler links the
.Ar C
library and, where the system keeps the math functions in a library
of their own
.Li "(libm of glibc\&)" ,
that library too, so that
.Li "sqrt, pow"
and
.Li sin
can be declared and called.
.Pp
.Ar A
.Li C
function may call an IMP routine back:
.Li LOC(ROUTINE\&)
is its address.
An IMP routine receives its arguments as addresses, which is what
.Li qsort
gives its comparison function:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
V IS 6 LONG;
V[0]:=5; V[1]:=2; V[2]:=9; V[3]:=1; V[4]:=7; V[5]:=3;
CCALL qsort(LOC(V),6,8,LOC(CMP));
(PRINT IGR 3,V[I]) FOR I TO 5;
PRINT /;
FINI(0);
SUBR CMP(A,B) IS A-B
%%
.Ed
.Pp
sorts the array and prints
.Li "  1  2  3  5  7  9" .
A routine that a
.Li C
function calls with arguments by value (a signal handler) receives each
value as the address in its parameter:
.Li LOC(A\&)
is the value that
.Ar C
passed.
To call a function through an address,
.Li "CCALL impcallp(F,A,...\&)"
calls the function at
.Ar F
with the arguments by value and has the result as a
.Ar C
.Li long ,
and
.Li "CCALL impcallw(F,A,...\&)"
has the result as an IMP word of 64 bits;
.Li "CCALL impcallw(LOC(TWICE),LOC(Y)\&)"
calls the IMP routine
.Ic TWICE
with the address of
.Ar Y .
.Ss "2.4.2 Being Called from C"
A
.Ic SUBR
is a
.Li C
function that takes pointers to 64-bit words and returns a 64-bit integer.
.Li "SUBR ADD2(A,B) IS A+B"
is declared in
.Li C
as
.Li "int64_t ADD2(int64_t *, int64_t *\&)" .
The header
.Li imp.h ,
installed in the
.Li include
directory, has the
.Li C
types of the words.
The symbol is the name as written; the routine is called with the address
of each argument.
For example, with the file
.Li lib.imp
.Bd -literal -offset indent
COUNT IS COMMON;
TABLE IS COMMON,4 LONG;
SUBR ADD2(A,B) IS A+B;
SUBR BUMP(N) IS (COUNT:=COUNT+N; COUNT);
SUBR GETT(I) IS TABLE[I]
%%
.Ed
.Pp
and the
.Li C
program
.Li main.c
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
extern int64_t COUNT;
extern int64_t TABLE[4];
int64_t ADD2(int64_t *, int64_t *);
int64_t BUMP(int64_t *);
int64_t GETT(int64_t *);
int main(void) {
    int64_t a = 3, b = 4, n = 10, i = 2;
    printf("%lld\en", (long long)ADD2(&a, &b));
    BUMP(&n); BUMP(&n);
    printf("%lld\en", (long long)COUNT);
    TABLE[2] = 7;
    printf("%lld\en", (long long)GETT(&i));
    return 0;
}
.Ed
.Pp
the commands
.Li "imp \-c lib.imp"
and
.Li "cc \-no\-pie \-o m main.c lib.o libimp.a"
make a program that prints 7, 20 and 7
.Li "(\-no\-pie is for a C compiler that makes position\-independent executables by default\&)" .
A file with no main program (no statements before the first
.Ic SUBR )
defines no
.Li impmain ,
so that it is linked into a
.Li C
program but not into a program that uses the main of the library (Section
2.4.4).
.Ss "2.4.3 COMMON and EXTERNAL Variables"
A variable declared
.Ic COMMON
is a global symbol of the object file, with the name as written, so
that
.Li C
declares it
.Li "extern int64_t X\&;"
(an array
.Li "A IS COMMON,8 LONG"
is
.Li "extern int64_t A[8]\&;" ) .
Every file of the program that uses it declares it
.Ic COMMON .
A variable that is not
.Ic COMMON
belongs to its file and
.Li C
cannot name it.
A
.Ic COMMON
variable that no routine of a file uses is not made by that file, as
the example above uses
.Ic TABLE
in
.Ic GETT .
.Pp
A
.Ic REAL
is an IEEE 754 double in a word that is the double’s own bits, so that
.Li C
declares a
.Li "COMMON REAL"
as
.Li "extern double X\&;" .
.Li C
code that handles the word of a
.Ic REAL
as an integer converts it with
.Li imp_real_to_double
and
.Li imp_double_to_real
of
.Li imp.h .
.Pp
.Li "X IS EXTERNAL"
declares a variable that is defined elsewhere, in the
.Li C
library for example.
Nothing is defined in the file, and
.Li LOC(X\&)
is the address of the variable that the linker finds.
The names that are not IMP names are written as special identifiers
(Section 2.2.1):
.Pp
.Li !environ\&!
is external; print stg 0,'environ is set: \&',igr 0,(!environ!
Ne
.Li 0),/ ;
.Pp
prints
.Li "environ is set: \-1" .
A
.Ic COMMON
variable or a
.Ic SUBR
with the name
.Li !a\-b\&!
is the symbol
.Li a_2db :
a byte that is not a letter, a digit or an underscore is written as
an underscore and two hexadecimal digits.
.Ss "2.4.4 Names, Programs and Command Line"
Names are case sensitive and a symbol is the name as written;
.Li "SUBR Add"
and
.Li "SUBR ADD"
are two functions.
The IMP names are the names of
.Ar C
(letters, digits and underscores), and the symbols of the runtime library
and the compiler are lower case
.Li "(impmain, imppr, imprd\&)"
or begin with
.Li RTL ;
a program should not use them for its own.
.Pp
The statements before the first
.Ic SUBR
of a file are the function
.Li impmain
of that file.
The library has a
.Li main
that calls
.Li impmain
with the command line kept for the program.
A program with a
.Li main
of its own (in
.Li C ,
or a
.Ic SUBR
called
.Li main )
does not link the
.Li main
of the library, and calls
.Li impmain
itself if it has one.
The command line is read with
.Li "CCALL impargc(0\&)" ,
the number of arguments, and
.Li "CCALL impargvp(0\&)" ,
the address of the array of pointers; the routine
.Li IMPARGET(OUT,V,I\&)
stores element I of such an array in the word at
.Ic OUT ,
so that
.Bd -literal -offset indent
N:=CCALL impargc(0);
IMPARGET(LOC(W),CCALL impargvp(0),0);
PRINT IGR 0,N,STG 0,[W],/;
FINI(0)
%%
.Ed
.Pp
prints the number of arguments and the program’s name.
Exactly one of the files of a program has statements before its first
.Ic SUBR .
.Ss "2.4.5 Callbacks and Function Pointers"
.Ar A
.Li C
function that takes a function pointer
.Li "(qsort, bsearch, signal, atexit\&)"
calls it with the arguments of
.Li C ,
by value and with the types of
.Li C ;
an
.Li IMP
routine takes addresses.
.Ar A
.Li CALLBACK
declaration gives a
.Li SUBR
the signature of the call, and
.Li CALLBACK(NAME\&)
is the address of a
.Li C
function that makes the call:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
CALLBACK INT CMP(PTR, PTR);
V IS 6 LONG;
V[0]:=5; V[1]:=2; V[2]:=9; V[3]:=1; V[4]:=7; V[5]:=3;
CCALL qsort(LOC(V),6,8,CALLBACK(CMP));
(PRINT IGR 3,V[I]) FOR I TO 5;
PRINT /;
FINI(0);
SUBR CMP(A,B) IS (
  [A]<[B] => RETURN 0-1;
  [A]=[B] => RETURN 0;
  1)
%%
.Ed
.Pp
sorts the array and prints the numbers 1 2 3 5 7 9, each in a field
of three.
The declaration has the form
.Li "CALLBACK result NAME(types\&)"
with the result and the types of Section 2.4.1
.Pf \&( Li CHAR
to
.Li "FLOAT, VOID"
for the result or as the whole list of parameters); it comes before
the
.Li SUBR ,
and a
.Li SUBR
with another number of parameters than the declaration is an error.
Each parameter reaches the routine as a word in a variable of its own,
converted as the result of a declared function is: a signed type is
sign-extended, an unsigned type and
.Li PTR
are zero-extended,
.Li DOUBLE
and
.Li FLOAT
are a
.Ic REAL .
A parameter declared
.Li PTR
is the address that
.Li C
passed, so
.Li A
is the address of an element of the array and
.Li [A\&]
is the element.
The value of the routine is converted to the type of the result: an
integer type takes the low bits of the word
.Li "(RETURN 0\-1 is the int \-1), DOUBLE"
and
.Li FLOAT
take a
.Ic REAL ,
and
.Li VOID
ignores it.
.Li CALLBACK(NAME\&)
of a name that is not declared a callback is an error, and so is
.Li "CCALL NAME(...\&)"
of one: a callback is called without
.Li CCALL ,
with addresses, as any
.Li SUBR
is.
.Pp
A callback is a thunk that the compiler writes into the object file
of the first
.Li CALLBACK(NAME\&)
in it; the routine itself can be in another file.
The variables of an
.Li IMP
routine are static, so a callback that is entered again while it runs
(a signal handler that interrupts the same routine, a comparison that
sorts) shares its variables with the first entry.
For example
.Bd -literal -offset indent
CALLBACK VOID CATCH(INT);
CALLBACK VOID BYE(VOID);
EXTERNAL PTR signal(INT, PTR);
EXTERNAL INT raise(INT);
EXTERNAL INT atexit(PTR);
EXTERNAL INT puts(PTR);
CCALL signal(2,CALLBACK(CATCH));
CCALL atexit(CALLBACK(BYE));
CCALL raise(2);
CCALL puts('after the signal');
FINI(0);
SUBR CATCH(S) IS CCALL puts('caught a signal');
SUBR BYE() IS CCALL puts('bye')
%%
.Ed
.Pp
prints
.Li "caught a signal, after the signal"
and
.Li bye .
.Pp
To call a
.Li C
function through a pointer that the program has (from
.Li dlsym ,
from a structure, or a
.Li CALLBACK(NAME\&) ) ,
.Li "FNPTR result NAME(types\&)"
declares the signature, and
.Li "CCALL NAME(F, A, ...\&)"
calls the function at the address
.Li F
with the arguments
.Li A ,
\&... converted to the types as for a declared function.
The function is the first argument and counts: a declaration of one
type takes two arguments.
Three dots after the last type declare a function of a variable number
of arguments.
For example
.Bd -literal -offset indent
CALLBACK DOUBLE HALVE(DOUBLE);
FNPTR DOUBLE HALVECALL(DOUBLE);
EXTERNAL INT printf(PTR, ...);
X IS REAL;
X:=CCALL HALVECALL(CALLBACK(HALVE),5.0);
CCALL printf('%.2f%c',X,10);
FINI(0);
SUBR HALVE(A) IS (
  Y IS REAL;
  Y:=A;
  Y:=Y/2.0;
  Y)
%%
.Ed
.Pp
prints
.Li 2.50 .
A callback has at most 16 parameters and is not variadic; a structure
by value is not a type (Section 2.4.6 has the layouts of structures
in memory).
.Ss "2.4.6 Structure Layouts"
A
.Ar C
structure is memory at an address.
.Ar A
.Li STRUCT
statement declares its layout, the names and the
.Ar C
types of its fields, and
.Li "FIELD(P, NAME.FIELD\&)"
reads and writes a field of the structure at the address
.Li P
with the type and at the offset that
.Ar C
gives it, in place of byte offsets written by hand:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
STRUCT PAIR (LONG SEC, LONG NSEC);
EXTERNAL PTR calloc(SIZE, SIZE);
T:=CCALL calloc(1,SIZEOF(PAIR));
FIELD(T,PAIR.SEC):=5;
FIELD(T,PAIR.NSEC):=0-7;
PRINT IGR 0,FIELD(T,PAIR.SEC),STG 0,' ',IGR 0,FIELD(T,PAIR.NSEC),STG 0,' ',IGR 0,OFFSETOF(PAIR,NSEC),/;
FINI(0)
%%
.Ed
.Pp
prints
.Li "5 \-7 8" .
The 8 is the offset of
.Li NSEC :
a
.Li long
is 8 bytes on a machine whose longs are 64 bits and 4 bytes on one whose
longs are 32 bits, and the layout is that of the machine the program
is compiled for
.Li "(\-\-target included\&)" .
.Pp
The declaration is
.Li "STRUCT name (type field, type field, ...\&)" .
A type is one of the types of section 2.4.1
.Li "(CHAR to FLOAT, not VOID\&)"
or a structure declared earlier in the same file, which makes the field
a nested structure.
.Li "type field[N\&]"
declares an array of
.Li N
elements,
.Li N
a constant.
The compiler places each field at the next multiple of its alignment,
as a
.Ar C
compiler does:
.Li CHAR
and
.Li UCHAR
take 1 byte,
.Li SHORT
and
.Li USHORT
2,
.Li "INT, UINT"
and
.Li FLOAT
4,
.Li "LLONG, ULLONG"
and
.Li DOUBLE
8, and
.Li "LONG, ULONG, SIZE"
and
.Li PTR
the width of a
.Ar C
.Li long ,
8 bytes or 4 where the pointers are 32 bits.
Each type aligns to its size, except that the 8 byte types align to
4 on the i386, as the System
.Ar V
ABI has it.
A structure aligns as its most aligned field and its size is rounded
up to that alignment.
Names of structures, like names of fields, are case sensitive, and a
declaration holds to the end of the file.
.Pp
.Li SIZEOF(name\&)
is the size in bytes of a structure or of a type of
.Ar C
.Pf \&( Li SIZEOF(PTR\&) ) ,
and
.Li "OFFSETOF(name, field\&)"
the offset of a field; both are constants.
A field of a nested structure is named by the path of its fields, separated
by dots:
.Li "OFFSETOF(name, outer.inner), FIELD(P, name.outer.inner\&)" .
.Pp
.Li "FIELD(P, name.field\&)"
is a value or a place to store to, in an expression, on the left of
.Li := ,
as an argument of
.Li CCALL .
As a value it has the type of the field converted as the result of a
declared function is: a signed type is sign-extended, an unsigned type
and
.Li PTR
are zero-extended,
.Li DOUBLE
and
.Li FLOAT
are a
.Li REAL .
A store converts to the type of the field: an integer field takes the
low bits of an integer and the integer part of a
.Li REAL ,
a
.Li DOUBLE
or
.Li FLOAT
field takes a
.Li REAL
or an integer converted to real.
A store changes the bytes of its field and no others.
.Li "FIELD(P, name.field, I\&)"
is element
.Li I
of a field that is an array; the index is not checked.
An array of structures is reached by adding the offset of the element
to the address:
.Li "FIELD(P+(I*SIZEOF(name)), name.field\&)" .
The address
.Li P
is any expression, a pointer from
.Li malloc ,
an address of a variable
.Pf \&( Li LOC(X\&) )
or a pointer that
.Ar C
returned.
For example, an array of structures sorted by
.Li qsort
with a comparison that reads their fields:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
STRUCT REC (INT KEY, DOUBLE W);
CALLBACK INT BYKEY(PTR, PTR);
EXTERNAL PTR calloc(SIZE, SIZE);
EXTERNAL VOID qsort(PTR, SIZE, SIZE, PTR);
N:=4;
A:=CCALL calloc(N,SIZEOF(REC));
((FIELD(A+(I*SIZEOF(REC)),REC.KEY):=(N-I)*10) FOR I IN 0,1,N-1);
CCALL qsort(A,N,SIZEOF(REC),CALLBACK(BYKEY));
((PRINT IGR 3,FIELD(A+(I*SIZEOF(REC)),REC.KEY)) FOR I IN 0,1,N-1);
PRINT /;
FINI(0);
SUBR BYKEY(X,Y) IS (
  FIELD(X,REC.KEY)<FIELD(Y,REC.KEY) => RETURN 0-1;
  FIELD(X,REC.KEY)=FIELD(Y,REC.KEY) => RETURN 0;
  1)
%%
.Ed
.Pp
prints
.Li "10 20 30 40" ,
each in a field of three.
The compiler reports at compile time a structure or a field that is
not declared, a name declared twice, a type that is not a type of
.Ar C
or a structure declared earlier, an array field named without an element
or a field that is not an array named with one, and a field of a structure
type used as a value.
Bit fields and unions are not declared, a structure is not passed or
returned by value, and the attributes that change a layout
.Li "(packed, aligned\&)"
are not known; the layout is what the program declares.
.Pp
The compiler reads no
.Ar C
header.
The tool
.Li imp\-cimport
does: it runs the
.Ar C
preprocessor on a header and writes the
.Li EXTERNAL
declarations of the functions, the
.Li STRUCT
declarations of the structures and the
.Li LET
declarations of the enumerators that it can express, and lists the rest
in a comment with the reason.
A declaration holds to the end of its file, so the declarations go in
front of the program:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp-cimport -n qsort -n strlen stdlib.h > decls.imp
cat decls.imp prog.imp > all.imp
imp all.imp
.Ed
.Pp
.Li "\-n NAME"
and
.Li "\-p PREFIX"
select names, and
.Li "\-D MACRO"
writes the integer value of a macro, which the
.Ar C
compiler evaluates.
The manual page
.Li imp\-cimport(1\&)
and
.Li docs/FFI.md
describe the tool.
.Sh "3 HOW TO COMPILE AND RUN IMP72 PROGRAMS"
.Ss "3.1 Compiling Programs"
The IMP compiler is called by the command
.Li imp ,
which takes the options of a
.Li C
compiler:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp [options] file.imp ...
imp -i [-s LETTERS] [file.imp ...]
.Ed
.Pp
A file named on the command line is a source file (any name;
.Li \&.imp
by custom) or an object file or library
.Li "(.o, .a, .so\&)"
to link.
A file of syntax and declarations (Section 3.4) is named with -C, as
the original’s
.Li /C
switch, and is read before each source file.
Each source file is compiled by itself, to an object file; the objects
are linked with the others named, and with the runtime library
.Li libimp.a ,
to the executable program.
The options are
.Bd -literal -offset indent
-c          Compile to an object file, file.o, and do not link.  With several sources
            there is one object for each, in the current directory.
-S          Compile to the assembly of the target, file.s.
-Sir        Compile to LLVM IR text, file.ll.
-o FILE     Name the output (a.out for a program).  With -c, -S or -Sir, one source only.
-C FILE     A file of syntax and declarations, read before the sources (in the order given).
-s LETTERS  Set the compiler switches (below).
-I IMAGE    Start from the compiler saved by /U or /V (Section 3.4).
-i          Read commands from the terminal (Section 3.1.2).
--run       Compile and run the program in the process of the compiler.
--target=TRIPLE  Compile for another system (Section 3.1.3).
--sysroot=DIR, --runtime=FILE   Where the files of that system are.
--version   Print the version (imp 0.10.0).
--help      Print the options.
.Ed
.Pp
The compiler finds its syntax file
.Li syntax.imc ,
the file of the
.Ar C
interface
.Li ffi.imc
and
.Li libimp.a
next to itself
.Li "(share/imp and lib/imp of its installation prefix\&)" ;
the environment variables IMP72SYNTAX, IMP72FFI and IMP72LIB name others.
It makes no temporary file for
.Li "\-c, \-S, \-Sir"
or
.Li \-\-run ;
to link, it hands the linker the object in an unnamed memory file on
.Ic LINUX ,
and elsewhere in a private directory (mode 0700) in
.Ic TMPDIR
if that is an absolute path, else in
.Li /tmp ,
deleted at exit, on an error and on a signal.
The messages go to the standard error.
A compilation with errors writes no output and exits with the status
1; a wrong command line exits with the status 2.
The compiler is made of LLVM and lld libraries and runs no other program,
except
.Li ld.lld
where its lld is not a library.
.Pp
If you want to compile the IMP program on file
.Li sum.imp ,
the simplest way is
.Li "imp sum.imp" ,
and
.Li \&./a.out
runs it.
.Li "imp \-\-run sum.imp"
compiles and runs it in one step.
.Li "imp \-c a.imp b.imp"
makes
.Li a.o
and
.Li b.o ,
and
.Li "imp main.imp lib.imp \-o prog"
compiles two files and links them; a variable that the two share is
declared
.Ic COMMON
in both (Section 2.1.4).
.Pp
The compiler switches are given as the letters of the argument of
.Li \-s ,
and the listing and printouts go to the standard error:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
A  Print the code the compiler produced, one line for each operation of the abstract machine
   (Section 2.3.3.1.2); this is not the assembly of the machine, which -S writes.
L  Print a source listing.
Y  Print the source program as it is compiled (as L, on the terminal).
.Ed
.Pp
The letters
.Ar B ,
.Ar F ,
.Ar G ,
.Ar I ,
.Ar M ,
.Ar N ,
.Ar P ,
.Ar S ,
.Ar T ,
.Ar X
and
.Ar Z
print tables of the compiler (the assembler’s debugging output, free
storage, the parse, the directory, the code tree, the connectivity matrices,
the semantics arrays, the syntax graph and statistics); they are of
use to the person who works on the compiler.
.Ss "3.1.1 The Compiler Listing"
The source listing of the compiler has a number at the left of each
line of code.
This indicates the level of parenthesis nesting at the beginning of
the line.
Parentheses contained within
.Li #
or
.Li \(aq
signs are not counted.
If the compiler detects an error, the listing from that point in the
source file on is written.
.Ss "3.1.2 Interactive Commands"
.Li "imp \-i"
reads commands from the standard input.
It prints its version and the date as a banner and an asterisk as the
prompt.
A command line has the format
.Bd -literal -offset indent
obj,list_file.ext/a/b
.Ed
.Pp
All fields are optional.
The source is read from
.Li file.ext ;
if the extension is omitted the compiler looks for a file with no extension,
then
.Li file.imp ,
then
.Li file.imc .
The object file is written on
.Li obj.o
(it defaults to
.Li file.o ) .
Listings that the switches ask for go on
.Li list.lst ,
or on
.Li file.lst
if no list file is named.
The
.Li _
that separates the list file from the source is not a letter in a command.
The switches
.Li "/A, /C, /H, /L, /U, /V"
and
.Li /Y
are given after the file.
.Pp
/a produce a listing of the code the compiler produced (as the switch
a above). /c continue after this file: the compiler prompts for another
command; without /c it ends. /h help: list the switches. /l produce
a source listing. /u exit to save the compiler: name/u writes the compiler,
with the syntax compiled so far, on name.img. /v the same as /u. /y
list the source program on the terminal as it is compiled.
.Pp
A command with no file name goes on with the current file.
A file that cannot be found is answered by
.Li "? FILE NOT FOUND" .
.Li /D
lists the debugging switches.
If the end of the input file is reached without the two
.Li %
signs, the message
.Ic PREMATURE END OF INPUT FILE
is printed (Section 3.2).
.Ss "3.1.3 Cross Compiling"
.Li \-\-target=TRIPLE
(the names of LLVM, with the vendor left out if desired:
.Li "aarch64\-linux\-gnu, riscv64\-linux\-musl, i686\-linux\-gnu, armv7\-linux\-gnueabihf, x86_64\-apple\-darwin" )
with
.Li "\-c, \-S"
or
.Li \-Sir
makes the object in the format of that system, with 32-bit or 64-bit
pointers as it has; the word of the program remains 64 bits.
The byte order of the target is the one that LLVM’s data layout of the
target machine gives, so a big endian target
.Li "(s390x\-linux\-gnu, powerpc\-linux\-gnu\&)"
is compiled on a little endian compiler and the reverse: a text constant
is the word of its characters in the order of the memory of the target.
The architecture, the operating system, the environment and the object
format of a triple are checked by LLVM’s own triple parser, so the set
follows the installed LLVM; the vendor is free text.
Where the LLVM
.Li C++
headers are not installed the check is lenient: it looks at the characters
only, and LLVM’s answer for the target decides.
A triple that fails the check gives the message
.Li "imp: unrecognized target \(dqTRIPLE\(dq"
with the part that is wrong, the status is 1 and no file is written.
To link for another system, the compiler must be told where the files
of that system are:
.Li \-\-sysroot=DIR
(its root, with
.Li "crt1.o, crti.o, crtn.o"
and the
.Li C
library under
.Li DIR/usr/lib ,
or for macOS the
.Ic SDK
directory, or for
.Ic WINDOWS
a
.Li mingw\-w64
tree with
.Li crt2.o
and
.Li libmingw32.a )
and
.Li \-\-runtime=FILE
(the
.Li libimp.a
built for it), or the environment variables IMP72SYSROOT and IMP72RUNTIME.
The dynamic linker and the
.Li \-m
emulation come from the triple, for about 25 architectures of
.Ic LINUX
.Li "(glibc and musl\&)" ,
and for
.Ic FREEBSD ,
.Ic NETBSD ,
.Ic OPENBSD ,
macOS,
.Li mingw\-w64
and
.Ic CYGWIN .
There is no runtime library for
.Ic WINDOWS
or
.Ic CYGWIN :
objects can be made, and a program that has its own
.Li main
and needs no runtime can be linked.
A target the driver cannot link is refused with the system named.
Without them the message says what is missing
.Pf \&( Li "no crt1.o under DIR/usr/lib/TRIPLE..\&." )
and how to give it, or compile with
.Li \-c
and link on the other system.
The runtime library of a system is built by the compiler itself, with
no
.Li C
compiler.
.Li \-\-run
is for the system the compiler runs on.
.Ss "3.2 Compilation Error Diagnostics"
The compiler reports on three classes of errors: fatal errors, errors,
and advisory errors.
Advisory errors indicate a condition which may not be an error but should
be brought to the programmer’s attention.
They are labeled
.Ic ADVISORY .
Fatal errors terminate compilation immediately.
Regular errors do not terminate compilation, but subsequent compilation
may be affected.
A message is written on the standard error as
.Pp
.Li **
error - nested subroutines.
.Li **
advisory - production duplicates one already in syntax
.Li **
.Pp
After an error no object file is written and the exit status is 1.
.Pp
The philosophy behind the error diagnostics in IMP presumes a certain
amount of maturity on the part of the programmer.
He is less restricted than in a language such as FORTRAN, but he is
also less protected from his own errors.
.Pp
The following are the error diagnostics provided by the compiler.
They are regular errors unless otherwise indicated.
.Bl -tag -width 7n -compact
.It Li "ATTEMPT TO EXECUTE UNDEFINED SEMANTIC ROUTINE (Fatal\&)"
A syntax statement named a semantic routine that does not exist (you
already got
.Ic UNDEFINED SEMANTIC ROUTINE
for that), and an instance of the production has been compiled.
.It Li "ANTECEDENT NOT A HARDWARE REGISTER IN SYNONYM DECLARATION"
In a declaration such as
.Ar A
.Ic IS
.Ar B ,
which makes A a synonym of the register
.Ar B ,
.Ar B
is not one of
.Li 0R
to
.Li 15R .
.It Li "BAD MODIFIER"
A semantic condition has an identifier with a bad modifier.
.It Li "BAD RESULT OF SEMANTICS; IGNORED (Advisory\&)"
A semantic routine has returned a value for a semantic part which is
not an object on the semantics routine stack.
This may or may not cause trouble later on.
.It Li "BAD TYPE IN SEMANTICS (Fatal\&)"
An entry of the semantics array has a type that the compiler does not
know.
This is a bug in the compiler or in a syntax file.
.It Li "CALCULATED CONSTANT IS SUBSCRIPTED (Advisory\&)"
An expression has been evaluated to a constant and now an attempt is
being made to subscript it.
Since the calculation yields only one word, this doesn’t make much sense.
.It Li "CANNOT GET CORE TO INITIALIZE FREE STORAGE (Fatal\&)"
The system did not give the compiler the address space it reserves for
its tables.
.It Li "CANT USE NONTERMINALS IN CASE ARG LIST"
The parameter list of a
.Ic CASE
contains something that is not a constant.
.It Li "CASE CONTAINS SUBEXPRESSION WHICH IS CASE BUT WONT GENERALIZE (Advisory\&)"
You are warned that a semantic condition in a
.Ic CASE
definition contains a subexpression which is also an instance of the
production in the syntax part.
The main expression will generalize to other instances of the
.Ic CASE ,
but the subexpression won’t.
.It Li "CASE DEFINED TWICE OR SAME NAME AS SEMANTIC ROUTINE"
A
.Ic CASE
may be defined in one place only.
.It Li "CASE UNDEFINED (Fatal\&)"
You tried to reference a
.Ic CASE
semantics, but the case was never defined.
.It Li "CODE GENERATION STACK DID NOT REDUCE TO ONE ITEM"
The program parsed correctly, but semantics for some of the program
could not be found.
If you added your own syntax in the program, check it.
.It Li "ERROR CORRECTOR GIVES UP. (Fatal\&)"
The error corrector was unable to correct a syntax error.
.It Li "ERROR CORRECTOR GIVES UP AT % (Fatal\&)"
The error corrector was unable to correct a syntax error, and read past
the
.Li %
sign while trying.
.It Li "EXPONENT OUT OF RANGE"
The power of ten of a floating point constant is too large or too small
for a double.
.It Li "FREE STORAGE EXHAUSTED (Fatal\&)"
Your program has demanded more space to compile than the compiler’s
tables hold.
If you had syntax errors, you may be able to compile when the errors
are repaired.
Otherwise, make your program smaller.
.It Li "FREE STORAGE UNLINKED (Fatal\&)"
A bug in the compiler.
.It Li "GO TO name, WHICH IS NOT IN THE SUBROUTINE"
A
.Ic GO TO
names a tag that is neither in the routine nor in the main program.
.It Li "ILLEGAL CHARACTER IN FLOATING CONSTANT"
A floating point constant has a character that is not a digit, the point,
the
.Li \(dq
or a minus sign.
.It Li "IN ADDCHAR \- NAME > 80 CHARACTERS"
A syntactic class name is too long.
.It Li "MODIFIER ON OTHER THAN FIRST OCCURRENCE OF NAME IS IGNORED"
In a semantic condition the modifiers belong to the first occurrence
of a name only.
.It Li "MORE THAN 2047 REGISTERS IN ONE COMPILATION (Fatal\&)"
The code of a source file names more than 2047 registers (temporaries
and the registers of the program).
Split the file.
.It Li "N NOT A CONSTANT IN N LONG"
.Ar N
may be a constant expression but must contain no variables (except those
syntactically defined as constants).
.It Li "NAME TOO LONG (Fatal\&)"
You used an identifier longer than 100 characters in a syntactic condition.
.It Li "NESTED CASE DEFINITIONS ILLEGAL"
A
.Ic CASE
was defined inside a
.Ic CASE .
.It Li "NESTED SUBROUTINES"
Due probably to mismatched parentheses, a subroutine definition has
been initiated before the previous one was closed out.
.It Li "NON\-CONSTANT EXPRESSION IN DATA STATEMENT"
The items of a
.Ic DATA
statement are constants and strings.
.It Li "NUMBER MISSING OR OUT OF PLACE IN SEMANTICS IDENTIFIER"
In a semantic conditional, you had a number in the wrong format or wrong
place.
.It Li "OPERATION ON A REAL CONSTANT"
The compiler does not evaluate that operation on real constants; put
the value in a variable first.
.It Li "OUT OF MEMORY FOR RELOCATION TABLE (Fatal\&)"
The compiler could not get memory for a table of the free storage.
.It Li "PREMATURE END OF INPUT FILE (Fatal\&)"
Compiler read the entire input file but did not encounter the
.Li %%
which terminates IMP programs.
Perhaps you: 1.
Only had one
.Li % .
2.
Failed to match
.Li #
or
.Li \(aq
with another one, so that the
.Li %
sign got absorbed in a constant/comment.
Check the numbers down the left margin of the listing to see when they
stopped following the parenthesis nesting of the code.
.It Li "PRODUCTION DUPLICATES ONE ALREADY IN SYNTAX (Advisory\&)"
The production in a syntax statement is identical to one already in
the syntax.
This is not an error, and is used to add special semantic cases to already
defined syntax.
The driver of the compiler does not print it.
.Pp
.Ic PROPERTY DECLARED IS NOT APPLICABLE TO REGISTER ,
.Ic PROPERTY MAY ONLY BE DECLARED FOR REGISTERS :
.Ic COMMON ,
.Ic REAL ,
.Ic INTEGER
or
.Ar N
.Ic LONG
was declared for a register, or
.Ic RELEASED
for a name that is not a register.
.It Li "PUSHDOWN STACK UNDERFLOW ERROR (Fatal\&)"
Indicates a compiler bug.
.It Li "QUOTED SEMANTICS NOT AN INSTANCE OF PRODUCTION"
A semantic condition was not an instance of the production in the syntax
statement in which it appeared.
.It Li "SEMANTIC CONDITION TOO COMPLEX (Fatal), SEMANTIC ROUTINES NESTED TOO DEEP (Fatal\&)"
A semantic condition or a nest of semantic routine calls beyond the
limits of the compiler.
.It Li "SEMANTICS STACK OVERFLOW (Fatal\&)"
There is exactly one major table in the compiler which is not dynamically
expandable, and your program has stuffed it full.
This can only happen through the use of a very complex semantic routine
call in a semantics part of a syntax statement, or maybe a bug in a
semantic routine, or a very long list of names in your program.
.It Li "STACK UNDERFLOW IN FSUB (Fatal\&)"
Indicates a compiler bug.
.It Li "SUBSCRIPTED REGISTER \- IGNORED"
The subscript on a register is disregarded.
.It Li "SYNTACTIC AMBIGUITY; UNRESOLVED AT %"
The parser has found a syntactic ambiguity which it was not able to
resolve by the end of your program.
If your program contains syntax statements, check them.
.It Li "SYNTACTIC AMBIGUITY"
The compiler was able to interpret a portion of your program in two
conflicting ways, and was not able to decide which way you had in mind.
The ambiguous segment of the program is indicated on the listing (after
a fashion) by a list of the identifiers (variable names and constants)
which appear in the segment, printed in reverse order.
Some constructs which may produce this error are:
.Li "A=>B=>C ELSE D" ,
which is really ambiguous and should be parenthesized explicitly, and
(A
.Ic RELOP
.Li B)=>C ,
where
.Ic RELOP
is any relational operator, which is not ambiguous, and will generate
correct code, but the parentheses may be removed to get rid of the error
message.
.It Li "SYNTAX ERROR"
The parser has not been able to interpret your program as a legal IMP
expression.
It has attempted to continue the compilation by replacing part of your
program at the point of the error by what it hopes is a correction of
the error.
It is possible that more errors will result from this correction later
on.
The offending line is written with a line feed inserted at the point
at which the error was detected, followed by the line
.Li **
.Ic SYNTAX ERROR
.Li **
and a mark under the place, and then the correction:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
X:=1 +
* 2; PRINT IGR 0,X,/; FINI(0)
** SYNTAX ERROR **
          ^
** SYNTAX ERROR - "* " IS REPLACED BY "0 * "
.Ed
.It Li "THE ADDRESS OF A COMPUTED CONSTANT"
.Ic LOC
was applied to a constant that is not a word of the program (a constant
expression).
.It Li "TOO MANY ARGS TO SEMANTIC RTN OR PARAM STACK UNDERFLOW (Fatal\&)"
Either you tried to execute a semantic routine with more than 10 arguments,
or else there is a compiler bug.
.It Li "TOO MANY ERRORS"
.Ic STOPPED ,
.Ic TOO MANY SYNTAX ERRORS
(Fatal): A large number of errors have been detected in your program,
and the compiler sees no point in proceeding.
.It Li "TOO MANY LOCAL SYMBOLS IN REFERENCED SEMANTICS (Fatal\&)"
You tried to reference quoted semantics with more than 10 local symbols.
.It Li "TWO LENGTHS IN SAME DECLARATION, TWO REGISTERS IN SAME DECLARATION"
A declaration with two
.Ar N
.Ic LONG ,
or with two synonyms for registers.
.It Li "UNDEFINED SEMANTIC ROUTINE"
A syntax statement calls a semantic routine that does not exist.
.It Li "UNKNOWN OPERATION IN THE CODE"
The code generator found an operation it cannot translate.
This is a bug in the compiler or in a syntax file.
.It Li "VALUE NOT A CONSTANT"
Value in
.Ic VALUE
semantics must be a constant.
.El
.Pp
The commands also print messages of their own:
.Li "imp: unknown option, imp: missing argument to, imp: cannot name the output with more than one source to compile, ? FILE NOT FOUND: name"
for a source that cannot be read,
.Li "imp: no target"
for a target that LLVM does not have, and the messages of the linker
(for example
.Li "undefined symbol: impmain"
when no file has a main program).
.Ss "3.3 Linking and Running"
The compiler links the program: its object files and those named on
the command line, the runtime library
.Li libimp.a
and the
.Li C
library, with lld in its own process.
The result is an executable of the system, which is run by its name.
For a program of several files:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
imp -c a.imp b.imp
imp a.o b.o -o prog
.Ed
.Pp
Exactly one of the files has a main program (statements before its first
.Ic SUBR ) ;
with none the link stops with
.Li "undefined symbol: impmain" .
The objects may be linked by another linker with the
.Li C
library and
.Li libimp.a
(Section 2.4.2).
If your program does any I/O, it should call
.Ic FINI
(see Section 2.2.7) to complete the I/O operations.
.Pp
If your program should fail at run time, it is a Unix process: an integer
division by zero or a reference to a bad address ends it with a signal,
and the files are not closed.
The program has no symbol table of its own;
.Li lldb
and
.Li gdb
use the symbols of the object, which are the names of the routines and
of the
.Ic COMMON
variables.
.Ss "3.4 Extended Compilers"
The language is defined by the syntax file the compiler reads: new syntax
is added by syntax statements (Section 2.3) in three ways.
.Bl -tag -width 3n -compact
.It 1.
In the source program itself: a syntax statement is a statement and
takes effect from where it is.
.It 2.
In a file named with -C on the command line, which is read before each
source file:
.Li "imp \-C inv.imc p1.imp" .
It ends with two
.Li %
signs, like a program.
The file
.Li ffi.imc
of the compiler, which defines
.Ic CCALL ,
.Ic EXTERNAL ,
.Ic CALLBACK ,
.Ic FNPTR
and
.Ic STRUCT ,
is such a file.
.It 3.
In an image of the compiler.
With
.Li "imp \-i" ,
the command
.Li inv.imc/C
reads the file and continues, and the command
.Li myc_/U
saves the compiler with the syntax read so far on
.Li myc.img .
A program is then compiled by the extended compiler with
.Li "imp \-I myc.img p1.imp" .
The image holds the tables of the compiler (the directory, the syntax,
the declarations) and the variables of its modules, and is for the compiler
that made it; another build refuses it.
It does not depend on the address at which the free storage of the compiler
lies: the compiler asks for the address the image was saved at, and
when the system gives another one (as a system that randomises the addresses
of a process does) the addresses of the tables that the modules hold
are moved with them.
.El
.Pp
The file
.Li syntax.imc
is the definition of IMP72, and the compiler reads it first.
To make a compiler with a different language, change the file (or point
IMP72SYNTAX at another) and the semantic routines it calls.
The compiler is built from its sources by
.Li make
in the source tree, which needs a C99 compiler and the LLVM libraries
(and lld, as a library, to link in the process).
.Li "make check"
runs the tests, and
.Li "make install PREFIX=DIR"
installs
.Li "bin/imp, share/imp/syntax.imc, share/imp/ffi.imc"
and
.Li lib/imp/libimp.a .
The sources of the compiler are in IMP72 (directory
.Li src )
and compile with the compiler (Section 4).
.Sh "4 INTERNAL DOCUMENTATION OF THE IMP72 COMPILER"
This section provides an overview of the organization of the IMP72 compiler.
Documentation of specific routines, data bases, etc., is included at
the beginning of each file of source code.
The intent of the present section is to provide a frame of reference
for the reader who wishes to tinker with the compiler.
For those with a more intellectual interest in the compiler’s workings
and design, [Bilofsky 1973], [Irons 1971] and [Weingart 1973] may be
more edifying.
.Pp
The compiler is programmed modularly wherever possible, at some cost
in efficiency.
(This cost has been substantially reduced by replacing many subroutine
calls by in-line syntactic macros.) This philosophy was adopted in order
to enable replacement and modification of portions of the compiler with
minimal repercussions.
Accordingly, there is no common data base in the compiler.
Necessary information concerning another module’s data base is acquired
via subroutine calls.
.Pp
The compiler may be divided conceptually into the following sections:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
